WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.985 align:middle line:90%


00:00:00.985 --> 00:00:02.440 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:02.440 --> 00:00:04.380 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:07.775 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:07.775 --> 00:00:09.540 align:middle line:90%


00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:14.800 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: So today, we're
going to talk about signaling.

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:19.480 align:middle line:84%
And what we did
last week was kind

00:00:19.480 --> 00:00:21.180 align:middle line:84%
of a simple example
of signaling,

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:23.720 align:middle line:84%
and today, we're going to do
a more involved application

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:25.040 align:middle line:90%
of signaling.

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:27.380 align:middle line:90%
So what do we mean by signaling?

00:00:27.380 --> 00:00:28.840 align:middle line:90%
What's the key idea here?

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:33.600 align:middle line:84%
Well, we're going to look at
games where we have two players.

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:40.040 align:middle line:90%
We have a sender and a receiver.

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:43.180 align:middle line:84%
And the sender has some
private information.

00:00:43.180 --> 00:00:48.280 align:middle line:90%


00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:50.600 align:middle line:84%
And then the sender, knowing
their private information,

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:52.995 align:middle line:84%
is going to make some
choice about an actions.

00:00:52.995 --> 00:00:54.620 align:middle line:84%
They're going to then
choose an action.

00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:57.480 align:middle line:90%


00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:00.360 align:middle line:84%
And we often call this
private information,

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:01.640 align:middle line:90%
maybe the sender's type.

00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:07.100 align:middle line:84%
This is what the sender knows
that the receiver doesn't know.

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:10.100 align:middle line:84%
And then the receiver is
going to observe the action

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:12.500 align:middle line:84%
that the sender
chooses, but they're not

00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:15.700 align:middle line:84%
going to directly observe the
sender's private information.

00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:28.500 align:middle line:84%
So the receiver observes
the action, but not type.

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:30.780 align:middle line:84%
So they see what action
the sender takes,

00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:33.500 align:middle line:84%
but they can't observe
the private information

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:35.580 align:middle line:90%
that the sender has.

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:37.940 align:middle line:84%
And then based
upon that, they're

00:01:37.940 --> 00:01:39.320 align:middle line:90%
also going to choose an action.

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:49.300 align:middle line:90%


00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:51.780 align:middle line:84%
So in the examples that
we're going to look at,

00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:54.940 align:middle line:84%
the receiver, when they're
choosing their action,

00:01:54.940 --> 00:01:58.280 align:middle line:84%
cares a lot about the
sender's private information.

00:01:58.280 --> 00:02:02.400 align:middle line:84%
And the receiver is going to
try to learn or infer something

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:04.520 align:middle line:84%
about the sender's
private information based

00:02:04.520 --> 00:02:07.080 align:middle line:84%
upon the action that
the sender chooses.

00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:09.800 align:middle line:84%
And conversely, the
sender cares a lot

00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:12.200 align:middle line:84%
about what the receiver
believes about them,

00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:15.200 align:middle line:84%
and the sender is going to
try to convey information

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:18.660 align:middle line:84%
about their private information
through their choice of action.

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:21.540 align:middle line:84%
Now that's a bit abstract, so
let's think of some examples.

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:26.000 align:middle line:90%


00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:28.920 align:middle line:84%
I don't know if any
of you come across--

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:31.500 align:middle line:84%
heard of the term
signaling used colloquially

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:34.260 align:middle line:84%
and heard of examples
where this term is applied.

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:37.400 align:middle line:90%


00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:38.540 align:middle line:90%
So a few examples.

00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:40.980 align:middle line:84%
I guess a classic one
would be luxury goods.

00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:43.680 align:middle line:90%


00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:46.840 align:middle line:84%
So here, the story would be
that the private information

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:49.560 align:middle line:84%
that the sender has, their
type is maybe how rich they

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:52.520 align:middle line:90%
are, how much money they have.

00:02:52.520 --> 00:02:58.660 align:middle line:84%
The action they're choosing
is what brand of bag to buy.

00:02:58.660 --> 00:03:01.260 align:middle line:84%
The receiver, then,
doesn't directly

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:03.220 align:middle line:84%
know how rich the
sender is, but they

00:03:03.220 --> 00:03:05.463 align:middle line:84%
do observe what brand
of item they choose.

00:03:05.463 --> 00:03:07.380 align:middle line:84%
And the receiver is going
to make an inference

00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:09.100 align:middle line:84%
about how rich the
person is based

00:03:09.100 --> 00:03:11.780 align:middle line:84%
upon how luxurious the
item is that they hold.

00:03:11.780 --> 00:03:15.080 align:middle line:84%
And the sender, wanting
to be perceived as rich,

00:03:15.080 --> 00:03:18.780 align:middle line:84%
will then choose to buy
something luxurious.

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:20.223 align:middle line:90%
Now, why does this work?

00:03:20.223 --> 00:03:21.640 align:middle line:84%
Well, your concern
might be, well,

00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:24.220 align:middle line:84%
look, if everyone wants
to be perceived as rich,

00:03:24.220 --> 00:03:27.422 align:middle line:84%
why doesn't everyone
just buy the luxury item?

00:03:27.422 --> 00:03:29.380 align:middle line:84%
And the issue is that,
well, they're expensive,

00:03:29.380 --> 00:03:31.000 align:middle line:84%
and only some people
can afford them.

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:32.980 align:middle line:84%
So a crucial aspect
for these signals

00:03:32.980 --> 00:03:35.692 align:middle line:84%
to be credible is that the
action people are taking

00:03:35.692 --> 00:03:37.900 align:middle line:84%
is generally going to have
a different cost depending

00:03:37.900 --> 00:03:39.200 align:middle line:90%
on their private information.

00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:41.140 align:middle line:84%
If everyone was able to buy
luxury items because they

00:03:41.140 --> 00:03:42.682 align:middle line:84%
were so cheap, that
wouldn't credibly

00:03:42.682 --> 00:03:43.767 align:middle line:90%
convey that I was wealthy.

00:03:43.767 --> 00:03:46.100 align:middle line:84%
But for a very expensive item,
because only sufficiently

00:03:46.100 --> 00:03:47.475 align:middle line:84%
wealthy people
can buy it, that's

00:03:47.475 --> 00:03:49.480 align:middle line:90%
going to convey information.

00:03:49.480 --> 00:03:52.960 align:middle line:84%
Another classic story from
the animal world, I guess,

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:57.270 align:middle line:90%
is a peacock's feathers.

00:03:57.270 --> 00:03:59.750 align:middle line:84%
Any ideas about what's
being conveyed here

00:03:59.750 --> 00:04:02.770 align:middle line:84%
and what's the role of
the peacock's feathers?

00:04:02.770 --> 00:04:06.110 align:middle line:90%


00:04:06.110 --> 00:04:11.010 align:middle line:84%
Why do peacocks grow very, very
ostentatious large feathers?

00:04:11.010 --> 00:04:14.090 align:middle line:84%
Well, it's not because people
really care about the feathers.

00:04:14.090 --> 00:04:16.310 align:middle line:84%
The idea would be that
this is a signal of health.

00:04:16.310 --> 00:04:19.110 align:middle line:84%
So if we map this into
our example, the sender,

00:04:19.110 --> 00:04:22.150 align:middle line:84%
one peacock privately
has private information

00:04:22.150 --> 00:04:23.670 align:middle line:90%
about how healthy they are.

00:04:23.670 --> 00:04:25.870 align:middle line:84%
They're trying to
attract maybe a mate,

00:04:25.870 --> 00:04:28.870 align:middle line:84%
and the mate cares about
how healthy the peacock is.

00:04:28.870 --> 00:04:31.650 align:middle line:84%
If the peacock grows
these enormous feathers,

00:04:31.650 --> 00:04:32.650 align:middle line:90%
that's extremely costly.

00:04:32.650 --> 00:04:33.730 align:middle line:90%
It takes a lot of energy.

00:04:33.730 --> 00:04:36.250 align:middle line:90%
You have to-- to grow this.

00:04:36.250 --> 00:04:38.855 align:middle line:84%
So by growing very, very
large, ostentatious feathers,

00:04:38.855 --> 00:04:40.230 align:middle line:84%
they're conveying
to the receiver

00:04:40.230 --> 00:04:41.810 align:middle line:84%
that I must be
very, very healthy.

00:04:41.810 --> 00:04:44.530 align:middle line:84%
And the receiver doesn't
care about the feathers,

00:04:44.530 --> 00:04:46.290 align:middle line:84%
they just care about
underlying health,

00:04:46.290 --> 00:04:49.310 align:middle line:84%
and the feathers provide
a signal of that.

00:04:49.310 --> 00:04:52.090 align:middle line:84%
One classic example, which
we'll go through today,

00:04:52.090 --> 00:04:55.930 align:middle line:90%
would be college.

00:04:55.930 --> 00:04:59.290 align:middle line:84%
So this is a classic
signaling application.

00:04:59.290 --> 00:05:05.130 align:middle line:84%
How does college fit into
this sender/receiver model,

00:05:05.130 --> 00:05:06.770 align:middle line:90%
or how could it?

00:05:06.770 --> 00:05:07.570 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:05:07.570 --> 00:05:11.328 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: --show a job recruiter
that you are aspiring--

00:05:11.328 --> 00:05:12.370 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Right, exactly.

00:05:12.370 --> 00:05:13.610 align:middle line:84%
So here, your
private information

00:05:13.610 --> 00:05:15.050 align:middle line:84%
might be how smart
you are, how diligent

00:05:15.050 --> 00:05:16.590 align:middle line:84%
you are, how
hardworking you are,

00:05:16.590 --> 00:05:18.850 align:middle line:84%
we could have various kind
of positive attributes.

00:05:18.850 --> 00:05:23.170 align:middle line:84%
You're choosing whether to go
to some difficult university

00:05:23.170 --> 00:05:25.210 align:middle line:90%
and get some demanding degree.

00:05:25.210 --> 00:05:27.770 align:middle line:84%
The receiver might
directly care about what

00:05:27.770 --> 00:05:29.937 align:middle line:84%
you learn in your degree,
but they might not.

00:05:29.937 --> 00:05:31.770 align:middle line:84%
It might be that your
degree is actually not

00:05:31.770 --> 00:05:34.137 align:middle line:84%
that relevant for what
you're doing on the job,

00:05:34.137 --> 00:05:35.970 align:middle line:84%
but they care about
your degree because it's

00:05:35.970 --> 00:05:37.427 align:middle line:90%
a signal of how smart you are.

00:05:37.427 --> 00:05:39.010 align:middle line:84%
The idea would be
that some people are

00:05:39.010 --> 00:05:42.053 align:middle line:84%
able to get this degree and
pass, and other people are not,

00:05:42.053 --> 00:05:43.970 align:middle line:84%
and therefore, your
ability to get this degree

00:05:43.970 --> 00:05:50.270 align:middle line:84%
and take this action reveals
information about your ability.

00:05:50.270 --> 00:05:55.978 align:middle line:84%
So let's dig in a little bit
deeper into this college example

00:05:55.978 --> 00:05:58.270 align:middle line:84%
because that's going to be
kind of the main application

00:05:58.270 --> 00:06:00.590 align:middle line:84%
we're going to look at
today, and this is often

00:06:00.590 --> 00:06:02.690 align:middle line:84%
goes under the name of
job market signaling.

00:06:02.690 --> 00:06:09.510 align:middle line:90%


00:06:09.510 --> 00:06:11.910 align:middle line:84%
So this was introduced
in this famous paper

00:06:11.910 --> 00:06:14.930 align:middle line:90%
by Michael Spence in 1973--

00:06:14.930 --> 00:06:17.770 align:middle line:84%
I think it was actually
his PhD dissertation,

00:06:17.770 --> 00:06:19.550 align:middle line:84%
and he ultimately
won the Nobel Prize

00:06:19.550 --> 00:06:21.388 align:middle line:84%
for this idea, which
we might go over today

00:06:21.388 --> 00:06:22.930 align:middle line:84%
and you might say
it's pretty simple.

00:06:22.930 --> 00:06:24.090 align:middle line:84%
How did you get the
Nobel Prize for that?

00:06:24.090 --> 00:06:26.090 align:middle line:84%
But I think things after
the fact seem simple,

00:06:26.090 --> 00:06:29.110 align:middle line:84%
but coming up with them
is often quite difficult.

00:06:29.110 --> 00:06:32.970 align:middle line:84%
So let's just observe some
facts about the world,

00:06:32.970 --> 00:06:34.330 align:middle line:90%
about college education.

00:06:34.330 --> 00:06:36.730 align:middle line:84%
There's something called
the college wage premium.

00:06:36.730 --> 00:06:40.788 align:middle line:90%


00:06:40.788 --> 00:06:43.330 align:middle line:84%
And this refers to the fact that
at least all over the world,

00:06:43.330 --> 00:06:44.890 align:middle line:84%
but let's focus in
the United States,

00:06:44.890 --> 00:06:47.550 align:middle line:84%
people who go to college or
complete a college degree tend

00:06:47.550 --> 00:06:49.570 align:middle line:84%
to earn more than
people who don't.

00:06:49.570 --> 00:06:51.690 align:middle line:84%
And this is something labor
economists study a lot

00:06:51.690 --> 00:06:53.590 align:middle line:84%
and try to give
different estimates.

00:06:53.590 --> 00:06:56.173 align:middle line:84%
You'll see a lot of estimates
floating around depending on how

00:06:56.173 --> 00:06:58.730 align:middle line:84%
they measure it or which
cohort or which kind of person

00:06:58.730 --> 00:07:01.410 align:middle line:84%
they're looking at, but maybe a
rough estimate in the US might

00:07:01.410 --> 00:07:07.490 align:middle line:84%
be something like maybe 500K
to a million is maybe kind

00:07:07.490 --> 00:07:09.190 align:middle line:84%
of the middle-of-the-road
estimates,

00:07:09.190 --> 00:07:10.630 align:middle line:84%
and this is in
lifetime earnings.

00:07:10.630 --> 00:07:15.827 align:middle line:90%


00:07:15.827 --> 00:07:16.910 align:middle line:90%
So what do I mean by this?

00:07:16.910 --> 00:07:18.785 align:middle line:84%
I mean, if you just look
at people in the US,

00:07:18.785 --> 00:07:20.730 align:middle line:84%
you look at those who
have college degrees,

00:07:20.730 --> 00:07:23.063 align:middle line:84%
and you compute the average
earnings of those people who

00:07:23.063 --> 00:07:25.271 align:middle line:84%
have college degrees, and
then you look at people who

00:07:25.271 --> 00:07:27.938 align:middle line:84%
don't have college degrees, and
you look at the average earnings

00:07:27.938 --> 00:07:30.350 align:middle line:84%
of those people over their
lifetime, and you compare it,

00:07:30.350 --> 00:07:32.570 align:middle line:84%
you see that people who
go to college, on average,

00:07:32.570 --> 00:07:35.877 align:middle line:84%
earn maybe 500K to $1 million
more over their lifetime.

00:07:35.877 --> 00:07:38.210 align:middle line:84%
As I said, there's a few
different estimates about this.

00:07:38.210 --> 00:07:41.210 align:middle line:90%
Now the question is why.

00:07:41.210 --> 00:07:43.730 align:middle line:90%
Any thoughts about why this is?

00:07:43.730 --> 00:07:48.350 align:middle line:84%
Why do people go to
college earn more?

00:07:48.350 --> 00:07:49.370 align:middle line:90%
You're here at college.

00:07:49.370 --> 00:07:52.790 align:middle line:84%
Maybe you partly want to earn
more by coming to college.

00:07:52.790 --> 00:07:54.410 align:middle line:90%
Any thoughts about why this is?

00:07:54.410 --> 00:07:57.190 align:middle line:90%


00:07:57.190 --> 00:08:00.790 align:middle line:84%
So maybe-- we often separate
this into two different stories.

00:08:00.790 --> 00:08:03.010 align:middle line:84%
So one is what we might
call a causal story.

00:08:03.010 --> 00:08:06.430 align:middle line:90%


00:08:06.430 --> 00:08:09.070 align:middle line:84%
I'll just say causal, we don't
have to put the word story.

00:08:09.070 --> 00:08:10.230 align:middle line:90%
So is it causal?

00:08:10.230 --> 00:08:11.710 align:middle line:84%
Is it causal, what
I mean is, let's

00:08:11.710 --> 00:08:14.030 align:middle line:84%
look at someone who
doesn't go to college.

00:08:14.030 --> 00:08:16.010 align:middle line:84%
If they chose to
instead go to college,

00:08:16.010 --> 00:08:17.430 align:middle line:90%
would their earnings go up?

00:08:17.430 --> 00:08:18.870 align:middle line:90%
That would mean it's causal.

00:08:18.870 --> 00:08:20.450 align:middle line:90%
Or maybe it's not causal.

00:08:20.450 --> 00:08:23.003 align:middle line:90%


00:08:23.003 --> 00:08:24.670 align:middle line:84%
Maybe someone who
doesn't go to college,

00:08:24.670 --> 00:08:26.503 align:middle line:84%
if they did go to
college, wouldn't actually

00:08:26.503 --> 00:08:27.510 align:middle line:90%
increase their earnings.

00:08:27.510 --> 00:08:30.030 align:middle line:84%
Now I think a lot of people
would look at this fact

00:08:30.030 --> 00:08:32.330 align:middle line:84%
and just immediately say,
oh, it must be causal.

00:08:32.330 --> 00:08:34.850 align:middle line:84%
If going to college-- if people
who go to college earn more,

00:08:34.850 --> 00:08:36.490 align:middle line:84%
then it must be that if I
choose to go to college,

00:08:36.490 --> 00:08:38.032 align:middle line:84%
that's going to
increase my earnings,

00:08:38.032 --> 00:08:39.963 align:middle line:84%
and this is kind of
a classic fallacy--

00:08:39.963 --> 00:08:41.630 align:middle line:84%
or potential fallacy
when people haven't

00:08:41.630 --> 00:08:44.110 align:middle line:84%
thought so carefully about
this data, and that's possible.

00:08:44.110 --> 00:08:45.005 align:middle line:90%
It could be causal.

00:08:45.005 --> 00:08:46.630 align:middle line:84%
But one explanation
is it's not causal.

00:08:46.630 --> 00:08:47.380 align:middle line:90%
How could that be?

00:08:47.380 --> 00:08:51.090 align:middle line:84%
How could it be that people who
go to college earn more money,

00:08:51.090 --> 00:08:53.990 align:middle line:84%
yet going to college wouldn't
actually increase my earning?

00:08:53.990 --> 00:08:57.610 align:middle line:90%
How could that story make sense?

00:08:57.610 --> 00:08:59.490 align:middle line:84%
Well, the story here
would be that, well,

00:08:59.490 --> 00:09:01.610 align:middle line:84%
the people who go to college are
very different from the people

00:09:01.610 --> 00:09:02.710 align:middle line:90%
who don't go to college.

00:09:02.710 --> 00:09:03.950 align:middle line:90%
You have two pools of people.

00:09:03.950 --> 00:09:05.830 align:middle line:84%
You have people who go to
college and people who don't.

00:09:05.830 --> 00:09:07.788 align:middle line:84%
People who go to college
are just smarter, more

00:09:07.788 --> 00:09:10.090 align:middle line:84%
ambitious on average or
have some other trait

00:09:10.090 --> 00:09:12.350 align:middle line:90%
that is hard to measure.

00:09:12.350 --> 00:09:15.083 align:middle line:84%
They do earn more, but it's
not because they go to college,

00:09:15.083 --> 00:09:16.750 align:middle line:84%
they just happen to
be going to college.

00:09:16.750 --> 00:09:19.070 align:middle line:84%
And this is, I guess, the
classic story of this.

00:09:19.070 --> 00:09:21.278 align:middle line:84%
This is a problem with all
these health studies where

00:09:21.278 --> 00:09:23.050 align:middle line:84%
they say people who
eat five almonds a day

00:09:23.050 --> 00:09:24.435 align:middle line:90%
live five years longer.

00:09:24.435 --> 00:09:25.810 align:middle line:84%
Eating five almonds
a day doesn't

00:09:25.810 --> 00:09:27.450 align:middle line:84%
make you live five years longer,
but people who eat almonds

00:09:27.450 --> 00:09:28.670 align:middle line:84%
are different in a
lot of different ways.

00:09:28.670 --> 00:09:29.830 align:middle line:84%
They eat healthily
in other ways,

00:09:29.830 --> 00:09:32.163 align:middle line:84%
they run, they exercise, they
do all these other things.

00:09:32.163 --> 00:09:36.332 align:middle line:84%
So it could be that though
there's a big earnings gap,

00:09:36.332 --> 00:09:37.790 align:middle line:84%
it's not because
you go to college,

00:09:37.790 --> 00:09:39.190 align:middle line:84%
it's just a spurious
correlation.

00:09:39.190 --> 00:09:41.820 align:middle line:84%
So that's one
potential explanation.

00:09:41.820 --> 00:09:43.900 align:middle line:84%
Another would be
that it is causal.

00:09:43.900 --> 00:09:46.640 align:middle line:84%
That if I, as an individual,
choose to go to college,

00:09:46.640 --> 00:09:49.220 align:middle line:84%
that's going to increase
my expected earning.

00:09:49.220 --> 00:09:51.820 align:middle line:84%
And I think the best evidence
suggests that both-- there's

00:09:51.820 --> 00:09:53.400 align:middle line:84%
an element of both
of these at play.

00:09:53.400 --> 00:09:57.940 align:middle line:84%
There's-- maybe this we
might call selection,

00:09:57.940 --> 00:10:00.353 align:middle line:84%
the people who go to college
are a selected group.

00:10:00.353 --> 00:10:02.020 align:middle line:84%
They're different on
average than people

00:10:02.020 --> 00:10:03.140 align:middle line:90%
who don't go to college.

00:10:03.140 --> 00:10:06.170 align:middle line:84%
And I frame this as
causal versus not causal.

00:10:06.170 --> 00:10:08.420 align:middle line:84%
I think the best evidence
suggests it's a bit of both.

00:10:08.420 --> 00:10:11.020 align:middle line:84%
That when we look at this
earning gap between people

00:10:11.020 --> 00:10:13.357 align:middle line:84%
who go to college and people
who don't, partly it's

00:10:13.357 --> 00:10:14.440 align:middle line:90%
attributable to selection.

00:10:14.440 --> 00:10:16.240 align:middle line:84%
Those people just
have different skills,

00:10:16.240 --> 00:10:18.360 align:middle line:84%
and partly it's a causal
effect of college.

00:10:18.360 --> 00:10:20.120 align:middle line:84%
But I want to drill
down within causal,

00:10:20.120 --> 00:10:22.900 align:middle line:84%
and I think there's actually
two different mechanisms that

00:10:22.900 --> 00:10:23.920 align:middle line:90%
could be at work here.

00:10:23.920 --> 00:10:26.320 align:middle line:84%
So let's focus on
the causal component.

00:10:26.320 --> 00:10:29.180 align:middle line:84%
Let's just suppose that it is
true that if I go to college,

00:10:29.180 --> 00:10:31.020 align:middle line:90%
my earnings go up.

00:10:31.020 --> 00:10:32.700 align:middle line:90%
Why might that be the case?

00:10:32.700 --> 00:10:36.160 align:middle line:84%
Why my going to college
cause me to earn more?

00:10:36.160 --> 00:10:36.660 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:10:36.660 --> 00:10:37.820 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: You're
able to learn skills

00:10:37.820 --> 00:10:38.960 align:middle line:90%
that employers might want.

00:10:38.960 --> 00:10:39.627 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Great.

00:10:39.627 --> 00:10:40.680 align:middle line:90%
So.

00:10:40.680 --> 00:10:42.680 align:middle line:84%
Certainly the story that
people who run colleges

00:10:42.680 --> 00:10:44.240 align:middle line:84%
would like to focus
on is this story,

00:10:44.240 --> 00:10:47.080 align:middle line:90%
that it's about learning skills.

00:10:47.080 --> 00:10:49.880 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes this is called
human capital formation,

00:10:49.880 --> 00:10:52.640 align:middle line:84%
that you go to
school, you learn how

00:10:52.640 --> 00:10:55.580 align:middle line:84%
to code, you learn how to
communicate with people,

00:10:55.580 --> 00:10:58.360 align:middle line:84%
you learn knowledge, information
about the world, you mature,

00:10:58.360 --> 00:11:00.878 align:middle line:84%
various things, and these make
you a more valuable employee.

00:11:00.878 --> 00:11:02.420 align:middle line:84%
They allow you to
be more productive.

00:11:02.420 --> 00:11:04.280 align:middle line:84%
So this is sometimes--
we talk about this

00:11:04.280 --> 00:11:05.580 align:middle line:90%
might be about productivity.

00:11:05.580 --> 00:11:08.520 align:middle line:90%


00:11:08.520 --> 00:11:10.480 align:middle line:84%
But another
possibility, which we're

00:11:10.480 --> 00:11:17.580 align:middle line:84%
going to focus on today--
learning, is signaling.

00:11:17.580 --> 00:11:22.400 align:middle line:90%


00:11:22.400 --> 00:11:25.400 align:middle line:84%
So the other possibility
could be that it is causal.

00:11:25.400 --> 00:11:27.188 align:middle line:84%
Going to college
makes me earn more,

00:11:27.188 --> 00:11:29.480 align:middle line:84%
but it's not because I actually
learn anything valuable

00:11:29.480 --> 00:11:30.380 align:middle line:90%
in college.

00:11:30.380 --> 00:11:33.360 align:middle line:84%
It's simply because going to
college proves to the employer

00:11:33.360 --> 00:11:36.220 align:middle line:84%
that I'm smart, and therefore,
the employer pays me more.

00:11:36.220 --> 00:11:37.140 align:middle line:90%
It's still causal.

00:11:37.140 --> 00:11:39.640 align:middle line:84%
If I didn't go to college,
I wouldn't earn as much.

00:11:39.640 --> 00:11:41.100 align:middle line:84%
So it is true that
going to college

00:11:41.100 --> 00:11:43.520 align:middle line:84%
changes my expected
earnings, but it changes it

00:11:43.520 --> 00:11:45.760 align:middle line:84%
not because I've actually
acquired valuable skills,

00:11:45.760 --> 00:11:47.510 align:middle line:84%
but because I've
demonstrated that I could

00:11:47.510 --> 00:11:49.220 align:middle line:84%
do a very difficult
task, and that makes

00:11:49.220 --> 00:11:50.520 align:middle line:90%
employers want to hire me.

00:11:50.520 --> 00:11:52.180 align:middle line:84%
So one way of
separating this, you

00:11:52.180 --> 00:11:58.142 align:middle line:84%
could imagine that a school--
in the extreme case, you

00:11:58.142 --> 00:12:00.100 align:middle line:84%
could imagine a school
that teaches skills that

00:12:00.100 --> 00:12:02.350 align:middle line:84%
really have no value, but
are just really, really hard

00:12:02.350 --> 00:12:03.260 align:middle line:90%
to learn.

00:12:03.260 --> 00:12:04.380 align:middle line:90%
And you might have--

00:12:04.380 --> 00:12:05.940 align:middle line:84%
people who graduate
from that school

00:12:05.940 --> 00:12:08.190 align:middle line:84%
might actually still do
well in the workforce.

00:12:08.190 --> 00:12:09.440 align:middle line:90%
And maybe an example of that--

00:12:09.440 --> 00:12:12.047 align:middle line:84%
I mean, I think athletics
are a valuable pursuit,

00:12:12.047 --> 00:12:14.380 align:middle line:84%
but you might think that
people who succeed in athletics

00:12:14.380 --> 00:12:17.422 align:middle line:84%
are often hired by firms
to work in finance, say.

00:12:17.422 --> 00:12:19.380 align:middle line:84%
It's not that being good
at running makes you--

00:12:19.380 --> 00:12:21.520 align:middle line:84%
or being good at weightlifting
makes you better at finance,

00:12:21.520 --> 00:12:23.060 align:middle line:84%
but it shows that you've
done something very hard that

00:12:23.060 --> 00:12:25.643 align:middle line:84%
reveals something maybe about
your diligence, your work ethic,

00:12:25.643 --> 00:12:28.780 align:middle line:84%
and that might be a reason
why firms want to hire you.

00:12:28.780 --> 00:12:30.822 align:middle line:84%
So in reality, I think
both are probably present,

00:12:30.822 --> 00:12:33.030 align:middle line:84%
and I think the best empirical
evidence at the moment

00:12:33.030 --> 00:12:35.200 align:middle line:84%
suggests both of these
mechanisms are at play,

00:12:35.200 --> 00:12:36.912 align:middle line:84%
but to highlight the
signaling aspect,

00:12:36.912 --> 00:12:38.620 align:middle line:84%
we're going to focus
all in on signaling,

00:12:38.620 --> 00:12:40.620 align:middle line:84%
and we're going to consider
a model of education

00:12:40.620 --> 00:12:42.780 align:middle line:84%
where education conveys
no valuable skills.

00:12:42.780 --> 00:12:45.940 align:middle line:84%
I want to say up front, this
is not my view of education,

00:12:45.940 --> 00:12:48.080 align:middle line:90%
but just to highlight this--

00:12:48.080 --> 00:12:50.560 align:middle line:84%
I think we all understand
how learning skills increases

00:12:50.560 --> 00:12:51.460 align:middle line:90%
your wage.

00:12:51.460 --> 00:12:54.100 align:middle line:84%
I think this is the novel
element of Spence's model,

00:12:54.100 --> 00:12:55.820 align:middle line:84%
and this is what
we want to look at.

00:12:55.820 --> 00:12:57.320 align:middle line:84%
And I will say,
there are people who

00:12:57.320 --> 00:12:59.613 align:middle line:84%
argue that this is
almost all of education.

00:12:59.613 --> 00:13:01.280 align:middle line:84%
It's a debate, but
there are some people

00:13:01.280 --> 00:13:03.363 align:middle line:84%
who say this is really all
that's what's going on.

00:13:03.363 --> 00:13:04.540 align:middle line:90%
So let's go through this.

00:13:04.540 --> 00:13:06.983 align:middle line:90%


00:13:06.983 --> 00:13:08.900 align:middle line:84%
So one-- I'll just maybe
make one observation,

00:13:08.900 --> 00:13:11.817 align:middle line:84%
one way that people try to
argue it's all about signaling,

00:13:11.817 --> 00:13:13.400 align:middle line:84%
one kind of interesting
empirical test

00:13:13.400 --> 00:13:15.942 align:middle line:84%
is they look at people who go
to college for three and a half

00:13:15.942 --> 00:13:17.497 align:middle line:90%
years and then drop out.

00:13:17.497 --> 00:13:19.080 align:middle line:84%
Now if it's really
about skills, you'd

00:13:19.080 --> 00:13:20.880 align:middle line:84%
think someone who goes for three
and a half years has acquired

00:13:20.880 --> 00:13:23.420 align:middle line:84%
almost all of the skills as
someone who goes for four years.

00:13:23.420 --> 00:13:24.780 align:middle line:84%
But if it's about
signaling, you might think,

00:13:24.780 --> 00:13:26.280 align:middle line:84%
well, the fact that
they dropped out

00:13:26.280 --> 00:13:28.180 align:middle line:84%
reveals a lot about who
they are as a person,

00:13:28.180 --> 00:13:29.920 align:middle line:84%
and indeed, people who drop
out just before graduating

00:13:29.920 --> 00:13:30.540 align:middle line:90%
earn a lot less.

00:13:30.540 --> 00:13:32.620 align:middle line:84%
And that's one argument for
why signaling is at play,

00:13:32.620 --> 00:13:34.100 align:middle line:84%
but we can debate
about these things.

00:13:34.100 --> 00:13:34.600 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:13:34.600 --> 00:13:39.480 align:middle line:84%
So now let's go through our
formal model of signaling.

00:13:39.480 --> 00:13:43.560 align:middle line:84%
So in our model we're going to
have a sender and a receiver,

00:13:43.560 --> 00:13:46.690 align:middle line:90%
just as I said before.

00:13:46.690 --> 00:13:48.440 align:middle line:84%
And we're really going
to simplify things.

00:13:48.440 --> 00:13:51.580 align:middle line:84%
We're going to imagine the
sender just has two types.

00:13:51.580 --> 00:13:57.400 align:middle line:84%
They privately know they're
type t, which is either l or h.

00:13:57.400 --> 00:13:59.660 align:middle line:84%
They're either a low
type or a high type.

00:13:59.660 --> 00:14:03.167 align:middle line:84%
And we're going
to think of this--

00:14:03.167 --> 00:14:05.000 align:middle line:84%
maybe we can think of
this a bit abstractly,

00:14:05.000 --> 00:14:10.380 align:middle line:84%
but a low type is someone who
is going to find education more

00:14:10.380 --> 00:14:13.295 align:middle line:84%
costly, and a high type is going
to find education less costly.

00:14:13.295 --> 00:14:14.920 align:middle line:84%
We can interpret this
in a lot of ways.

00:14:14.920 --> 00:14:16.543 align:middle line:84%
It could be about
your intelligence,

00:14:16.543 --> 00:14:17.960 align:middle line:84%
it could be about
your work ethic,

00:14:17.960 --> 00:14:20.863 align:middle line:84%
it could be about the quality of
the school you went to before.

00:14:20.863 --> 00:14:22.780 align:middle line:84%
There's a lot of things
that can go into this,

00:14:22.780 --> 00:14:24.660 align:middle line:84%
but we're just going to
quite abstractly call

00:14:24.660 --> 00:14:26.720 align:middle line:90%
it a low type and a high type.

00:14:26.720 --> 00:14:31.340 align:middle line:90%


00:14:31.340 --> 00:14:34.360 align:middle line:84%
And what they're
going to choose is

00:14:34.360 --> 00:14:38.080 align:middle line:84%
they're going to choose
a level of education.

00:14:38.080 --> 00:14:41.480 align:middle line:84%
And in reality, education kind
of usually comes in in units.

00:14:41.480 --> 00:14:44.680 align:middle line:84%
Like, you can't-- we don't
really think you get 862 days

00:14:44.680 --> 00:14:45.260 align:middle line:90%
of education.

00:14:45.260 --> 00:14:47.160 align:middle line:84%
We usually think you either
get-- graduate high school

00:14:47.160 --> 00:14:49.140 align:middle line:84%
or you graduate college,
but to make things simple,

00:14:49.140 --> 00:14:50.848 align:middle line:84%
we're going to make
education continuous,

00:14:50.848 --> 00:14:53.880 align:middle line:84%
e, and we're just going to say
it's a number between 0 and 1.

00:14:53.880 --> 00:14:57.680 align:middle line:90%
So you can think of this--

00:14:57.680 --> 00:15:02.280 align:middle line:84%
just a stylized model where you
can get any continuous amount

00:15:02.280 --> 00:15:02.900 align:middle line:90%
of education.

00:15:02.900 --> 00:15:07.760 align:middle line:84%
This could be something like the
fraction of your life you spend

00:15:07.760 --> 00:15:10.000 align:middle line:90%
being educated or something.

00:15:10.000 --> 00:15:12.040 align:middle line:84%
Now the receiver,
we're going to think

00:15:12.040 --> 00:15:18.360 align:middle line:84%
of this as maybe a firm
or a market of firms.

00:15:18.360 --> 00:15:24.760 align:middle line:84%
And they're going to
observe e, but not t.

00:15:24.760 --> 00:15:27.980 align:middle line:84%
When a firm chooses to hire you,
they see what education you got.

00:15:27.980 --> 00:15:30.080 align:middle line:84%
They don't directly
observe this type t,

00:15:30.080 --> 00:15:32.220 align:middle line:84%
whether you're a low
or high type person.

00:15:32.220 --> 00:15:33.920 align:middle line:84%
And then they're going
to choose a wage.

00:15:33.920 --> 00:15:38.420 align:middle line:90%


00:15:38.420 --> 00:15:41.067 align:middle line:84%
In reality, I think a
lot of individual firms,

00:15:41.067 --> 00:15:42.900 align:middle line:84%
the wage is roughly set
and they're choosing

00:15:42.900 --> 00:15:43.760 align:middle line:90%
whether to hire you or not.

00:15:43.760 --> 00:15:46.332 align:middle line:84%
There's maybe some flexibility
over wages, but that's one way

00:15:46.332 --> 00:15:47.040 align:middle line:90%
we can think of--

00:15:47.040 --> 00:15:49.457 align:middle line:84%
I think it's helpful to think
of this kind of as a market.

00:15:49.457 --> 00:15:52.340 align:middle line:84%
So it may be that each
individual firm is making

00:15:52.340 --> 00:15:54.143 align:middle line:84%
a binary choice about
whether to hire you,

00:15:54.143 --> 00:15:56.560 align:middle line:84%
but then you go to the firm
that gives you the best offer.

00:15:56.560 --> 00:16:00.900 align:middle line:84%
So in the end, the outcome of
the firm-- of the labor market

00:16:00.900 --> 00:16:03.280 align:middle line:84%
is that you get hired at
some place for some wage,

00:16:03.280 --> 00:16:04.660 align:middle line:84%
and depending on
how well you do,

00:16:04.660 --> 00:16:07.620 align:middle line:84%
that wage is determined
by which firm hires you.

00:16:07.620 --> 00:16:09.820 align:middle line:90%
So now let's talk about payoffs.

00:16:09.820 --> 00:16:11.820 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to
assume the sender,

00:16:11.820 --> 00:16:19.100 align:middle line:90%
their payoffs are like this.

00:16:19.100 --> 00:16:21.660 align:middle line:84%
So the sender wants
to get paid more.

00:16:21.660 --> 00:16:24.687 align:middle line:84%
Their payoff is-- their utility
is increasing their wage.

00:16:24.687 --> 00:16:26.020 align:middle line:90%
They don't like going to school.

00:16:26.020 --> 00:16:27.800 align:middle line:84%
Again, another maybe
stylized assumption,

00:16:27.800 --> 00:16:30.230 align:middle line:84%
some people enjoy going to
school and that's why they go,

00:16:30.230 --> 00:16:31.970 align:middle line:84%
but we're not going to
have that in our model.

00:16:31.970 --> 00:16:34.345 align:middle line:84%
We're going to assume that
people just don't like school.

00:16:34.345 --> 00:16:36.970 align:middle line:84%
And notice, the more they get
educated, the more costly it is.

00:16:36.970 --> 00:16:38.950 align:middle line:84%
So they'd always
rather have less school

00:16:38.950 --> 00:16:41.430 align:middle line:84%
than more school, other
things being equal.

00:16:41.430 --> 00:16:44.610 align:middle line:84%
But now their type t appears
in the denominator here--

00:16:44.610 --> 00:16:46.390 align:middle line:84%
and maybe I should
be more precise.

00:16:46.390 --> 00:16:50.010 align:middle line:84%
Here, we have b, a natural
if we did it any other way,

00:16:50.010 --> 00:16:52.430 align:middle line:84%
the low type is lower
than the high type.

00:16:52.430 --> 00:16:55.030 align:middle line:84%
So by putting t in
the denominator here,

00:16:55.030 --> 00:16:58.350 align:middle line:84%
what I mean is if you're a
high type, if t is higher,

00:16:58.350 --> 00:17:00.430 align:middle line:84%
then the coefficient
in front of this cost

00:17:00.430 --> 00:17:03.450 align:middle line:84%
is lower, and
therefore, the cost,

00:17:03.450 --> 00:17:05.790 align:middle line:84%
or more importantly, the
marginal cost of education

00:17:05.790 --> 00:17:08.869 align:middle line:90%
is lower for the high type.

00:17:08.869 --> 00:17:10.190 align:middle line:90%
Why might that be true?

00:17:10.190 --> 00:17:12.190 align:middle line:84%
Well, maybe they
don't have to study

00:17:12.190 --> 00:17:13.837 align:middle line:90%
as hard to get into the school.

00:17:13.837 --> 00:17:15.670 align:middle line:84%
Maybe once they get in,
it's easier for them

00:17:15.670 --> 00:17:17.337 align:middle line:84%
to get good grades
and graduate, there's

00:17:17.337 --> 00:17:19.790 align:middle line:84%
many stories we can
tell, but, again, we're

00:17:19.790 --> 00:17:22.750 align:middle line:84%
going to think of your type
as representing how costly it

00:17:22.750 --> 00:17:25.473 align:middle line:90%
is for you to get education.

00:17:25.473 --> 00:17:26.890 align:middle line:84%
And then the
receiver, we're going

00:17:26.890 --> 00:17:29.070 align:middle line:84%
to use a bit of a weird
payoff specification.

00:17:29.070 --> 00:17:33.650 align:middle line:90%


00:17:33.650 --> 00:17:36.890 align:middle line:84%
The receiver's utility
is going to be like this.

00:17:36.890 --> 00:17:38.750 align:middle line:84%
Now, this may at first
seem a bit strange.

00:17:38.750 --> 00:17:40.542 align:middle line:84%
What we're saying is
the receiver's utility

00:17:40.542 --> 00:17:43.130 align:middle line:84%
is the negative of the
difference between the wage they

00:17:43.130 --> 00:17:47.330 align:middle line:84%
actually charge and the type
of the applicant of the sender.

00:17:47.330 --> 00:17:49.410 align:middle line:84%
And really, the reason
we make that assumption

00:17:49.410 --> 00:17:57.010 align:middle line:84%
is the implication of this
is that the optimal wage w is

00:17:57.010 --> 00:18:02.770 align:middle line:90%
equal to the expectation of t.

00:18:02.770 --> 00:18:06.010 align:middle line:84%
And this is just an algebraic
fact about the quadratic loss

00:18:06.010 --> 00:18:07.810 align:middle line:84%
function, you can check
this with algebra.

00:18:07.810 --> 00:18:10.970 align:middle line:84%
So if I don't know what t is,
with some probability, it's low;

00:18:10.970 --> 00:18:12.970 align:middle line:84%
with some probability,
it's high.

00:18:12.970 --> 00:18:17.170 align:middle line:84%
The optimal wage for me to set
is exactly my expectation of t.

00:18:17.170 --> 00:18:20.090 align:middle line:84%
So if I know the person is low
ability, it's optimal for me

00:18:20.090 --> 00:18:21.490 align:middle line:90%
to set a wage of l.

00:18:21.490 --> 00:18:24.210 align:middle line:84%
If I know they're a high
type, h, it's optimal for me

00:18:24.210 --> 00:18:25.410 align:middle line:90%
to set a wage of h.

00:18:25.410 --> 00:18:27.990 align:middle line:84%
And if I think it's a 50/50,
then I want to pay them exactly

00:18:27.990 --> 00:18:29.395 align:middle line:90%
1/2 l plus 1/2 h.

00:18:29.395 --> 00:18:30.770 align:middle line:84%
Now you might say,
wait a second,

00:18:30.770 --> 00:18:31.812 align:middle line:90%
that's not what firms do.

00:18:31.812 --> 00:18:33.630 align:middle line:84%
They pay the lowest
wage they can subject

00:18:33.630 --> 00:18:35.250 align:middle line:84%
to getting the
worker, but again, we

00:18:35.250 --> 00:18:37.250 align:middle line:84%
can think of this as the
outcome of competition.

00:18:37.250 --> 00:18:39.270 align:middle line:84%
So if there's many
firms competing,

00:18:39.270 --> 00:18:42.590 align:middle line:84%
if I charge-- if I pay a
worker less than their expected

00:18:42.590 --> 00:18:46.062 align:middle line:84%
ability, then maybe I'm going
to lose out to another firm

00:18:46.062 --> 00:18:47.770 align:middle line:84%
and the other firm is
going to hire them.

00:18:47.770 --> 00:18:49.290 align:middle line:84%
If I pay them more than
their expected ability,

00:18:49.290 --> 00:18:50.332 align:middle line:90%
then I'm overpaying them.

00:18:50.332 --> 00:18:53.630 align:middle line:84%
So we can think of this as a
reduced form representation

00:18:53.630 --> 00:18:56.830 align:middle line:90%
of a competitive labor market.

00:18:56.830 --> 00:18:59.690 align:middle line:84%
And I should say something
about the prior here.

00:18:59.690 --> 00:19:03.510 align:middle line:84%
Let's assume that h occurs
with probability pi and l

00:19:03.510 --> 00:19:06.230 align:middle line:90%
with 1 minus p.

00:19:06.230 --> 00:19:10.150 align:middle line:84%
So in the population, fraction
pi of people are of type h,

00:19:10.150 --> 00:19:12.870 align:middle line:84%
are the high types, and
fraction 1 minus pi of people

00:19:12.870 --> 00:19:14.010 align:middle line:90%
are the low types.

00:19:14.010 --> 00:19:19.070 align:middle line:90%


00:19:19.070 --> 00:19:22.450 align:middle line:84%
OK, so now we're ready
to analyze this game.

00:19:22.450 --> 00:19:26.173 align:middle line:84%
And the first step is always to
write down what a strategy is.

00:19:26.173 --> 00:19:28.090 align:middle line:84%
Notice, this game is a
little more complicated

00:19:28.090 --> 00:19:30.490 align:middle line:84%
than what we looked at
before because now, education

00:19:30.490 --> 00:19:31.530 align:middle line:90%
is something continuous.

00:19:31.530 --> 00:19:34.425 align:middle line:84%
Before in the class on Thursday,
we looked at this problem

00:19:34.425 --> 00:19:36.550 align:middle line:84%
where you chose, whether
to submit an essay or not,

00:19:36.550 --> 00:19:37.830 align:middle line:90%
and that was a binary choice.

00:19:37.830 --> 00:19:39.210 align:middle line:90%
I'll make one other observation.

00:19:39.210 --> 00:19:40.752 align:middle line:84%
Last Thursday, we
looked at something

00:19:40.752 --> 00:19:44.170 align:middle line:84%
where your private type
affected how much you wanted

00:19:44.170 --> 00:19:46.570 align:middle line:84%
to get into a school,
but not how costly it

00:19:46.570 --> 00:19:48.127 align:middle line:90%
was to submit an essay.

00:19:48.127 --> 00:19:49.710 align:middle line:84%
Now we're doing an
opposite assumption

00:19:49.710 --> 00:19:52.750 align:middle line:84%
where your type doesn't affect
how much you want to get paid.

00:19:52.750 --> 00:19:55.090 align:middle line:84%
Everyone wants to get paid
more, but your type only

00:19:55.090 --> 00:19:57.350 align:middle line:84%
affects how costly it is
for you to get educated,

00:19:57.350 --> 00:19:59.670 align:middle line:84%
so it's kind of the inverse
of what we did last week.

00:19:59.670 --> 00:20:03.210 align:middle line:84%
And I think this is probably a
pretty good model of the labor

00:20:03.210 --> 00:20:07.130 align:middle line:84%
market where most people want
more higher wages, that's not

00:20:07.130 --> 00:20:08.970 align:middle line:90%
really what distinguishes them.

00:20:08.970 --> 00:20:11.770 align:middle line:84%
OK, so what is a strategy,
or more precisely, what

00:20:11.770 --> 00:20:14.890 align:middle line:90%
is an assessment?

00:20:14.890 --> 00:20:17.690 align:middle line:84%
So remember, an assessment is
a strategy profile together

00:20:17.690 --> 00:20:19.130 align:middle line:90%
with a belief system.

00:20:19.130 --> 00:20:24.990 align:middle line:84%
So we need to first say, let's
look at the sender strategy.

00:20:24.990 --> 00:20:27.150 align:middle line:84%
What is the strategy
for the sender?

00:20:27.150 --> 00:20:30.350 align:middle line:84%
Well, the sender chooses an
action, a level of education

00:20:30.350 --> 00:20:33.310 align:middle line:84%
after knowing or learning
their private type t.

00:20:33.310 --> 00:20:36.430 align:middle line:84%
So we need to specify what
education the sender chooses

00:20:36.430 --> 00:20:38.750 align:middle line:84%
when they're the low type
and what education they

00:20:38.750 --> 00:20:40.030 align:middle line:90%
choose when they're high type.

00:20:40.030 --> 00:20:43.930 align:middle line:84%
So we'll represent a sender
strategy as el, comma, eh.

00:20:43.930 --> 00:20:45.190 align:middle line:90%
It's two numbers.

00:20:45.190 --> 00:20:47.470 align:middle line:84%
el says if you're
a low type sender,

00:20:47.470 --> 00:20:49.570 align:middle line:84%
this is how much education
I'm going to get.

00:20:49.570 --> 00:20:51.990 align:middle line:84%
eh says if I'm a
high type sender,

00:20:51.990 --> 00:20:55.790 align:middle line:84%
this is how much education
I'm going to get.

00:20:55.790 --> 00:20:59.390 align:middle line:84%
And yeah, I say sender, you can
think of this as the student.

00:20:59.390 --> 00:21:02.850 align:middle line:84%
OK, what is the strategy
for the receiver?

00:21:02.850 --> 00:21:08.430 align:middle line:90%


00:21:08.430 --> 00:21:09.670 align:middle line:90%
This is a bit harder.

00:21:09.670 --> 00:21:12.710 align:middle line:84%
Remember, a strategy is always
a complete contingent plan.

00:21:12.710 --> 00:21:15.310 align:middle line:84%
It says what action do you
take as a function of what

00:21:15.310 --> 00:21:17.030 align:middle line:90%
you observe.

00:21:17.030 --> 00:21:19.370 align:middle line:84%
So in this case, the
receiver observes your level

00:21:19.370 --> 00:21:21.570 align:middle line:84%
of education, and
they're going to choose

00:21:21.570 --> 00:21:24.350 align:middle line:84%
what wage they set as a
function of your education.

00:21:24.350 --> 00:21:29.850 align:middle line:84%
So formerly, the receiver is
choosing a function w from 0, 1,

00:21:29.850 --> 00:21:36.450 align:middle line:90%
maybe I'll say to 0, infinity.

00:21:36.450 --> 00:21:44.170 align:middle line:84%
So what this says is it assigns
to each level of education

00:21:44.170 --> 00:21:49.590 align:middle line:84%
e a wage w of e that the
receiver or firm will pay you

00:21:49.590 --> 00:21:52.370 align:middle line:84%
if they observe that your
education level is e.

00:21:52.370 --> 00:21:55.690 align:middle line:84%
And they have to do that for
every single education level

00:21:55.690 --> 00:21:58.570 align:middle line:90%
0, 1.

00:21:58.570 --> 00:22:00.790 align:middle line:84%
So that describes
a strategy profile,

00:22:00.790 --> 00:22:02.930 align:middle line:84%
but we're not done yet
because an assessment also

00:22:02.930 --> 00:22:04.430 align:middle line:90%
specifies beliefs.

00:22:04.430 --> 00:22:06.790 align:middle line:84%
So now we also have to
specify a belief system.

00:22:06.790 --> 00:22:13.050 align:middle line:90%


00:22:13.050 --> 00:22:16.270 align:middle line:84%
Well, the sender already
knows their type,

00:22:16.270 --> 00:22:18.860 align:middle line:84%
so we don't need to say anything
about the sender's type.

00:22:18.860 --> 00:22:20.980 align:middle line:84%
We have to specify
the receiver's beliefs

00:22:20.980 --> 00:22:22.940 align:middle line:90%
about the sender's type.

00:22:22.940 --> 00:22:25.380 align:middle line:84%
And once again,
the receiver might

00:22:25.380 --> 00:22:27.420 align:middle line:84%
learn about the sender's
type through the level

00:22:27.420 --> 00:22:28.780 align:middle line:90%
of education e.

00:22:28.780 --> 00:22:31.840 align:middle line:84%
So a belief system is going
to say, what do I as the firm

00:22:31.840 --> 00:22:35.320 align:middle line:84%
believe about you as a function
of what education I see?

00:22:35.320 --> 00:22:37.360 align:middle line:84%
If I see that you've
got a lot of education,

00:22:37.360 --> 00:22:39.040 align:middle line:84%
what do I believe
about your ability?

00:22:39.040 --> 00:22:41.120 align:middle line:84%
If I see that you didn't
get very much education,

00:22:41.120 --> 00:22:42.662 align:middle line:84%
what do I believe
about your ability?

00:22:42.662 --> 00:22:45.727 align:middle line:90%
So a belief system formerly--

00:22:45.727 --> 00:22:47.560 align:middle line:84%
maybe if we write it--
let's start formally,

00:22:47.560 --> 00:22:49.185 align:middle line:84%
and then we'll be a
little less formal.

00:22:49.185 --> 00:22:57.220 align:middle line:90%


00:22:57.220 --> 00:22:59.060 align:middle line:84%
So if we're formal about
it, a belief system

00:22:59.060 --> 00:23:01.220 align:middle line:84%
says for every
level of education

00:23:01.220 --> 00:23:04.140 align:middle line:84%
I see, I'm going to
form a belief which

00:23:04.140 --> 00:23:06.620 align:middle line:84%
is a probability distribution
over your ability,

00:23:06.620 --> 00:23:07.520 align:middle line:90%
either l or h.

00:23:07.520 --> 00:23:09.220 align:middle line:84%
So the notation
here just means this

00:23:09.220 --> 00:23:12.380 align:middle line:84%
is the set of probability
distributions over your ability

00:23:12.380 --> 00:23:14.660 align:middle line:84%
being l and your
ability being h.

00:23:14.660 --> 00:23:19.240 align:middle line:84%
But we can specify a
probability distribution here

00:23:19.240 --> 00:23:21.960 align:middle line:84%
just by saying, what do I think
is the probability that you're

00:23:21.960 --> 00:23:22.845 align:middle line:90%
a high type?

00:23:22.845 --> 00:23:25.220 align:middle line:84%
Because once we specify the
probability you're high type,

00:23:25.220 --> 00:23:27.845 align:middle line:84%
then the probability that you're
low type is just 1 minus that.

00:23:27.845 --> 00:23:30.560 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to
think of this--

00:23:30.560 --> 00:23:33.140 align:middle line:90%
maybe we'll write b h e--

00:23:33.140 --> 00:23:38.720 align:middle line:90%


00:23:38.720 --> 00:23:40.520 align:middle line:90%
so we'll just use this notation.

00:23:40.520 --> 00:23:43.480 align:middle line:84%
And this says, if I see
that your education is e,

00:23:43.480 --> 00:23:46.240 align:middle line:84%
this is the probability that
I think you're a high type.

00:23:46.240 --> 00:23:47.918 align:middle line:84%
And that fully
specifies my beliefs

00:23:47.918 --> 00:23:49.960 align:middle line:84%
because the probability
I think you're a low type

00:23:49.960 --> 00:23:51.060 align:middle line:90%
is always 1 minus that.

00:23:51.060 --> 00:23:54.120 align:middle line:84%
So maybe I'll just
write that because b

00:23:54.120 --> 00:23:57.920 align:middle line:84%
l given e, the probability that
I assign to you being a low type

00:23:57.920 --> 00:24:01.520 align:middle line:90%
given e, is just 1 minus b h e.

00:24:01.520 --> 00:24:04.240 align:middle line:90%


00:24:04.240 --> 00:24:06.580 align:middle line:84%
So technically, we have to
specify both of these things,

00:24:06.580 --> 00:24:08.372 align:middle line:84%
but we're only going
to specify one of them

00:24:08.372 --> 00:24:12.600 align:middle line:84%
because it's pinned
down by the other.

00:24:12.600 --> 00:24:13.420 align:middle line:90%
Great.

00:24:13.420 --> 00:24:17.540 align:middle line:84%
So now, we have to fill
all these things in.

00:24:17.540 --> 00:24:21.047 align:middle line:84%
We have to specify the sender
strategy, the receiver strategy,

00:24:21.047 --> 00:24:23.380 align:middle line:84%
and then the belief system,
which is a system of beliefs

00:24:23.380 --> 00:24:24.200 align:middle line:90%
for the receiver.

00:24:24.200 --> 00:24:25.400 align:middle line:90%
Any questions on this?

00:24:25.400 --> 00:24:31.660 align:middle line:90%


00:24:31.660 --> 00:24:33.160 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:24:33.160 --> 00:24:38.776 align:middle line:90%


00:24:38.776 --> 00:24:40.740 align:middle line:90%
Now what do we need to check?

00:24:40.740 --> 00:24:43.320 align:middle line:84%
So now we're going to look at
our equilibrium conditions.

00:24:43.320 --> 00:24:51.500 align:middle line:90%


00:24:51.500 --> 00:24:53.920 align:middle line:84%
And remember, we're
looking for the conditions,

00:24:53.920 --> 00:24:55.700 align:middle line:90%
and these are for pbE.

00:24:55.700 --> 00:24:59.100 align:middle line:84%
Our equilibrium notion is
perfect Bayesian equilibrium.

00:24:59.100 --> 00:25:03.240 align:middle line:84%
And we have three conditions or,
depending on how we write it,

00:25:03.240 --> 00:25:07.760 align:middle line:84%
one is sequential optimality
or sequential rationality.

00:25:07.760 --> 00:25:16.160 align:middle line:90%


00:25:16.160 --> 00:25:17.540 align:middle line:90%
You might see both terms used.

00:25:17.540 --> 00:25:19.457 align:middle line:84%
Sequential optimality,
sequential rationality,

00:25:19.457 --> 00:25:20.740 align:middle line:90%
they mean the same thing.

00:25:20.740 --> 00:25:22.100 align:middle line:90%
And then belief consistency.

00:25:22.100 --> 00:25:32.960 align:middle line:90%


00:25:32.960 --> 00:25:34.600 align:middle line:84%
And it's helpful
to separate this

00:25:34.600 --> 00:25:43.000 align:middle line:84%
into sequential
optimality for the sender,

00:25:43.000 --> 00:25:45.000 align:middle line:90%
and also for the receiver.

00:25:45.000 --> 00:25:47.440 align:middle line:84%
So let's first just say
what each of these things

00:25:47.440 --> 00:25:50.680 align:middle line:90%
mean in words.

00:25:50.680 --> 00:25:52.240 align:middle line:84%
Sequential optimality
for the sender

00:25:52.240 --> 00:25:56.520 align:middle line:84%
just means if I'm the
sender, and I know my type t,

00:25:56.520 --> 00:25:58.760 align:middle line:84%
my equilibrium
level of education

00:25:58.760 --> 00:26:01.400 align:middle line:84%
is optimal for me given
what everything else that's

00:26:01.400 --> 00:26:02.760 align:middle line:90%
happening in the game.

00:26:02.760 --> 00:26:09.820 align:middle line:84%
So for the sender, this means
for each t where, remember,

00:26:09.820 --> 00:26:11.820 align:middle line:84%
t is my type, and it could
be either low ability

00:26:11.820 --> 00:26:12.820 align:middle line:90%
or high ability.

00:26:12.820 --> 00:26:21.740 align:middle line:84%
So for each t, if my
type is t, choosing et--

00:26:21.740 --> 00:26:23.500 align:middle line:84%
remember, this could
either be el or eh

00:26:23.500 --> 00:26:34.140 align:middle line:84%
depending on the value
of t, is optimal given w.

00:26:34.140 --> 00:26:38.460 align:middle line:84%
Remember, what is w here? w is
the wage that the receiver sets.

00:26:38.460 --> 00:26:40.220 align:middle line:84%
And which education
level is optimal

00:26:40.220 --> 00:26:43.753 align:middle line:84%
for me depends on w because
what I'm thinking, what if I

00:26:43.753 --> 00:26:45.920 align:middle line:84%
go to school a little bit
less or a little bit more,

00:26:45.920 --> 00:26:47.018 align:middle line:90%
what if I deviate?

00:26:47.018 --> 00:26:48.560 align:middle line:84%
Well, to see whether
that's worth it,

00:26:48.560 --> 00:26:50.760 align:middle line:84%
I have to trade off
the cost of, say,

00:26:50.760 --> 00:26:52.860 align:middle line:84%
additional education
against the effect that

00:26:52.860 --> 00:26:54.460 align:middle line:90%
would have on my wage.

00:26:54.460 --> 00:26:56.700 align:middle line:84%
And that's going to be
governed by the strategy w

00:26:56.700 --> 00:26:57.720 align:middle line:90%
that the receiver uses.

00:26:57.720 --> 00:27:00.020 align:middle line:84%
So that's going to be
relevant in determining

00:27:00.020 --> 00:27:03.420 align:middle line:90%
whether et is optimal.

00:27:03.420 --> 00:27:06.540 align:middle line:90%
And then for the receiver--

00:27:06.540 --> 00:27:10.400 align:middle line:84%
well, with the receiver,
this is actually--

00:27:10.400 --> 00:27:13.560 align:middle line:84%
we can write this maybe
a bit more easily.

00:27:13.560 --> 00:27:17.640 align:middle line:90%
So for each e--

00:27:17.640 --> 00:27:24.840 align:middle line:84%
and e is in 0 or 1,
the receiver's wage

00:27:24.840 --> 00:27:27.060 align:middle line:84%
must be optimal
given their beliefs.

00:27:27.060 --> 00:27:42.960 align:middle line:84%
So we need choosing w of
e is optimal given b h e.

00:27:42.960 --> 00:27:44.720 align:middle line:84%
So what we're saying
is I'm the receiver.

00:27:44.720 --> 00:27:47.680 align:middle line:90%
I observe an education level e.

00:27:47.680 --> 00:27:52.200 align:middle line:84%
That causes me to form beliefs
b h given e about your type.

00:27:52.200 --> 00:27:54.160 align:middle line:84%
And given those
beliefs, the wage

00:27:54.160 --> 00:27:57.600 align:middle line:84%
that I actually choose as
the receiver is optimal.

00:27:57.600 --> 00:27:58.860 align:middle line:90%
Can we write that out in math?

00:27:58.860 --> 00:28:01.600 align:middle line:84%
I mean, given what
we said above,

00:28:01.600 --> 00:28:04.520 align:middle line:84%
we said that it's always
optimal given my quadratic loss

00:28:04.520 --> 00:28:08.780 align:middle line:84%
utility to always charge
a wage equal to my updated

00:28:08.780 --> 00:28:10.660 align:middle line:90%
expectation about your type.

00:28:10.660 --> 00:28:13.360 align:middle line:84%
So can we write this
condition in words?

00:28:13.360 --> 00:28:18.600 align:middle line:84%
What does it mean for w of e to
be optimal given b of h given e?

00:28:18.600 --> 00:28:21.180 align:middle line:90%


00:28:21.180 --> 00:28:22.600 align:middle line:90%
What must w of e be?

00:28:22.600 --> 00:28:30.360 align:middle line:90%


00:28:30.360 --> 00:28:30.860 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:28:30.860 --> 00:28:32.038 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Expected value 2.

00:28:32.038 --> 00:28:32.580 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: OK.

00:28:32.580 --> 00:28:36.060 align:middle line:84%
And how can we write that
in terms of b of h given e?

00:28:36.060 --> 00:28:39.800 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Um--

00:28:39.800 --> 00:28:40.300 align:middle line:90%


00:28:40.300 --> 00:28:42.220 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: So you're right.

00:28:42.220 --> 00:28:43.540 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Wait, are all--

00:28:43.540 --> 00:28:44.480 align:middle line:90%
other numbers.

00:28:44.480 --> 00:28:48.860 align:middle line:90%
So then the l top were--

00:28:48.860 --> 00:28:53.280 align:middle line:84%
Oh, is it h times b of h
plus l times minus 1 minus--

00:28:53.280 --> 00:28:54.280 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Exactly right.

00:28:54.280 --> 00:28:54.540 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:28:54.540 --> 00:28:56.240 align:middle line:84%
So indeed, it is the
expectation of t,

00:28:56.240 --> 00:28:59.140 align:middle line:84%
but we have to be careful
because our expectation of t

00:28:59.140 --> 00:29:01.760 align:middle line:84%
is influenced by the education
we see and our beliefs.

00:29:01.760 --> 00:29:03.840 align:middle line:84%
So it's not just our
prior expectation.

00:29:03.840 --> 00:29:06.170 align:middle line:84%
And maybe I should be clear
here, when I write this,

00:29:06.170 --> 00:29:08.730 align:middle line:90%
this is the updated expectation.

00:29:08.730 --> 00:29:12.590 align:middle line:90%


00:29:12.590 --> 00:29:14.110 align:middle line:84%
This is the
expectation of t that

00:29:14.110 --> 00:29:16.350 align:middle line:84%
reflects the new
information that I have

00:29:16.350 --> 00:29:17.690 align:middle line:90%
given your educational choice.

00:29:17.690 --> 00:29:19.425 align:middle line:84%
And indeed, if
seeing e caused me

00:29:19.425 --> 00:29:21.550 align:middle line:84%
to believe that this is
the probability that you're

00:29:21.550 --> 00:29:24.150 align:middle line:84%
a high type and 1 minus that
is the probability that you're

00:29:24.150 --> 00:29:26.670 align:middle line:84%
a low type, well, now I can
compute the expected value

00:29:26.670 --> 00:29:27.830 align:middle line:90%
of your type.

00:29:27.830 --> 00:29:31.270 align:middle line:84%
And, yeah, I should emphasize
here that l and h are actually

00:29:31.270 --> 00:29:31.770 align:middle line:90%
numbers.

00:29:31.770 --> 00:29:34.290 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes we think of high type
and low type as just labels,

00:29:34.290 --> 00:29:37.863 align:middle line:84%
but here, a low type is like
0.7 and a high type is like 2.3,

00:29:37.863 --> 00:29:39.530 align:middle line:84%
and these numbers
actually have meaning.

00:29:39.530 --> 00:29:40.390 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:29:40.390 --> 00:29:41.030 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:29:41.030 --> 00:29:48.110 align:middle line:84%
So indeed, we get h times p of h
given e plus l times-- well, we

00:29:48.110 --> 00:29:50.870 align:middle line:84%
could write b of l given e, but
using the notation we're using,

00:29:50.870 --> 00:29:55.790 align:middle line:84%
we'll just write 1
minus b of h given e.

00:29:55.790 --> 00:29:59.190 align:middle line:84%
So that's pretty
easy to write down.

00:29:59.190 --> 00:30:02.970 align:middle line:90%
And now, belief consistency.

00:30:02.970 --> 00:30:05.730 align:middle line:84%
Well, remember,
belief consistency

00:30:05.730 --> 00:30:08.050 align:middle line:84%
says the receiver
has to be applying

00:30:08.050 --> 00:30:10.970 align:middle line:90%
Bayes' rule wherever possible.

00:30:10.970 --> 00:30:13.650 align:middle line:84%
And the receiver can
only apply Bayes' rule

00:30:13.650 --> 00:30:15.290 align:middle line:84%
when they see a
level of education

00:30:15.290 --> 00:30:17.130 align:middle line:84%
that someone is actually
supposed to choose

00:30:17.130 --> 00:30:18.850 align:middle line:90%
with positive probability.

00:30:18.850 --> 00:30:22.170 align:middle line:90%
So we can separate--

00:30:22.170 --> 00:30:25.450 align:middle line:84%
sometimes this is called
on-path and off-path.

00:30:25.450 --> 00:30:28.930 align:middle line:90%


00:30:28.930 --> 00:30:30.330 align:middle line:90%
Let's say e prime.

00:30:30.330 --> 00:30:32.130 align:middle line:84%
So if they see an
education level

00:30:32.130 --> 00:30:35.450 align:middle line:84%
e that's on-path, meaning
that either the low type

00:30:35.450 --> 00:30:37.570 align:middle line:84%
or the high type is
supposed to choose this

00:30:37.570 --> 00:30:40.750 align:middle line:84%
with positive probability under
their equilibrium strategy,

00:30:40.750 --> 00:30:41.870 align:middle line:90%
then we apply Bayes' rule.

00:30:41.870 --> 00:30:51.330 align:middle line:90%


00:30:51.330 --> 00:30:53.390 align:middle line:84%
But if we see an
off-path education,

00:30:53.390 --> 00:30:55.410 align:middle line:84%
we see a level of
education that is not

00:30:55.410 --> 00:30:59.410 align:middle line:84%
supposed to be chosen
by either type,

00:30:59.410 --> 00:31:03.810 align:middle line:84%
and then there are
no restrictions.

00:31:03.810 --> 00:31:07.830 align:middle line:90%


00:31:07.830 --> 00:31:11.030 align:middle line:84%
And let's be a bit more precise
here, which education levels are

00:31:11.030 --> 00:31:13.870 align:middle line:84%
on-path given the
strategy that we've

00:31:13.870 --> 00:31:16.270 align:middle line:90%
written down for the sender?

00:31:16.270 --> 00:31:18.150 align:middle line:84%
Well, there's only
two that are on-path.

00:31:18.150 --> 00:31:20.550 align:middle line:84%
It's exactly the
numbers el and eh.

00:31:20.550 --> 00:31:23.470 align:middle line:84%
Those are the only two education
levels that the receiver expects

00:31:23.470 --> 00:31:25.310 align:middle line:90%
to see in equilibrium.

00:31:25.310 --> 00:31:31.962 align:middle line:84%
So these are precisely the cases
where e is either el or eh.

00:31:31.962 --> 00:31:33.670 align:middle line:84%
If the sender was
using a mixed strategy,

00:31:33.670 --> 00:31:34.930 align:middle line:84%
this would be a bit
more complicated.

00:31:34.930 --> 00:31:37.270 align:middle line:84%
There may be more education
levels they might choose.

00:31:37.270 --> 00:31:41.070 align:middle line:84%
But I, as the receiver, know
that, well, if you're low type,

00:31:41.070 --> 00:31:43.028 align:middle line:84%
you're supposed to choose
this education level;

00:31:43.028 --> 00:31:44.528 align:middle line:84%
if you're high type,
you're supposed

00:31:44.528 --> 00:31:47.310 align:middle line:84%
to choose this education level;
and if I see any other education

00:31:47.310 --> 00:31:50.170 align:middle line:84%
level, well, I wasn't supposed
to see this in equilibrium,

00:31:50.170 --> 00:31:51.730 align:middle line:84%
so I'm not able to
apply Bayes' rule.

00:31:51.730 --> 00:31:53.480 align:middle line:84%
Of course, this is
quite a simplification.

00:31:53.480 --> 00:31:55.890 align:middle line:84%
In reality, we see a
lot of education levels,

00:31:55.890 --> 00:31:58.550 align:middle line:84%
people's types are not binary,
and we'll do extensions later,

00:31:58.550 --> 00:32:01.610 align:middle line:84%
but for now, this
is where we are.

00:32:01.610 --> 00:32:06.870 align:middle line:84%
And then we have e is not
an element of el and eh.

00:32:06.870 --> 00:32:14.490 align:middle line:90%


00:32:14.490 --> 00:32:16.990 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:32:16.990 --> 00:32:19.870 align:middle line:84%
So to start dealing
with Bayes' rule,

00:32:19.870 --> 00:32:22.213 align:middle line:84%
it turns out that it's
helpful to distinguish

00:32:22.213 --> 00:32:23.630 align:middle line:84%
two different kinds
of equilibria,

00:32:23.630 --> 00:32:25.422 align:middle line:84%
and that's what we're
going to look at now.

00:32:25.422 --> 00:32:30.130 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to consider
two kinds of equilibria what

00:32:30.130 --> 00:32:42.150 align:middle line:84%
are called separating equilibria
and a pooling equilibria.

00:32:42.150 --> 00:32:50.170 align:middle line:90%


00:32:50.170 --> 00:32:53.050 align:middle line:84%
So what defines a
separating equilibria

00:32:53.050 --> 00:32:58.443 align:middle line:90%
is that el is not equal to eh.

00:32:58.443 --> 00:32:59.610 align:middle line:90%
So let's think through this.

00:32:59.610 --> 00:33:00.443 align:middle line:90%
What does this mean?

00:33:00.443 --> 00:33:01.710 align:middle line:90%
What does the receiver learn?

00:33:01.710 --> 00:33:05.030 align:middle line:84%
If we're in an equilibrium
where the low type chooses

00:33:05.030 --> 00:33:07.110 align:middle line:84%
one education level
and the high type

00:33:07.110 --> 00:33:08.790 align:middle line:84%
chooses a different
education level,

00:33:08.790 --> 00:33:11.402 align:middle line:84%
what can the receiver
conclude when they see

00:33:11.402 --> 00:33:12.610 align:middle line:90%
the sender's education level?

00:33:12.610 --> 00:33:13.410 align:middle line:90%
What do they learn?

00:33:13.410 --> 00:33:17.750 align:middle line:90%


00:33:17.750 --> 00:33:20.430 align:middle line:84%
Well, they perfectly
learn the sender's type

00:33:20.430 --> 00:33:23.910 align:middle line:84%
because in a
separating equilibrium,

00:33:23.910 --> 00:33:25.210 align:middle line:90%
the implication of that--

00:33:25.210 --> 00:33:29.510 align:middle line:90%


00:33:29.510 --> 00:33:31.510 align:middle line:90%
let's write it this way.

00:33:31.510 --> 00:33:34.830 align:middle line:84%
If I'm the receiver, and I see
that you chose a high level--

00:33:34.830 --> 00:33:37.230 align:middle line:84%
that you chose eh,
I know for certain

00:33:37.230 --> 00:33:38.530 align:middle line:90%
that you must be a high type.

00:33:38.530 --> 00:33:41.790 align:middle line:90%


00:33:41.790 --> 00:33:43.610 align:middle line:90%
And conversely, 0 here.

00:33:43.610 --> 00:33:44.110 align:middle line:90%
Why?

00:33:44.110 --> 00:33:47.670 align:middle line:84%
Well, if I see eh, the
low type never chooses eh.

00:33:47.670 --> 00:33:50.150 align:middle line:84%
They always choose a
different education level, el.

00:33:50.150 --> 00:33:52.930 align:middle line:84%
So if I see eh, I know that
you must be a high type,

00:33:52.930 --> 00:33:54.490 align:middle line:84%
and therefore, I
assign probability 1

00:33:54.490 --> 00:33:55.930 align:middle line:90%
to you being a high type.

00:33:55.930 --> 00:33:58.450 align:middle line:84%
Conversely, if I see
a low education level,

00:33:58.450 --> 00:34:00.330 align:middle line:84%
then I know that the
only kind of person

00:34:00.330 --> 00:34:02.455 align:middle line:84%
who would choose a low
level of education, at least

00:34:02.455 --> 00:34:04.210 align:middle line:84%
in this equilibrium,
is the low type,

00:34:04.210 --> 00:34:07.250 align:middle line:84%
so I assign 0 probability
to being the high type.

00:34:07.250 --> 00:34:11.670 align:middle line:84%
And then what about b of
h given e for all other e?

00:34:11.670 --> 00:34:16.330 align:middle line:90%


00:34:16.330 --> 00:34:20.850 align:middle line:84%
What about for e that
aren't equal to el or eh?

00:34:20.850 --> 00:34:22.650 align:middle line:84%
Well, there's no
restriction on these.

00:34:22.650 --> 00:34:24.429 align:middle line:90%
So these are unconstrained.

00:34:24.429 --> 00:34:26.449 align:middle line:84%
And we'll fill
these in as we want,

00:34:26.449 --> 00:34:28.070 align:middle line:84%
but this is going
to be unconstrained.

00:34:28.070 --> 00:34:33.409 align:middle line:90%


00:34:33.409 --> 00:34:36.210 align:middle line:84%
OK, now let's look at
the pooling equilibrium

00:34:36.210 --> 00:34:39.010 align:middle line:90%
where el equals eh.

00:34:39.010 --> 00:34:41.690 align:middle line:84%
And sometimes it's nice
to give a label to this.

00:34:41.690 --> 00:34:44.570 align:middle line:90%
So let's call this e star.

00:34:44.570 --> 00:34:46.730 align:middle line:84%
So here, both the high
types and the low types

00:34:46.730 --> 00:34:50.489 align:middle line:84%
choose the same level
of education, e star.

00:34:50.489 --> 00:34:55.000 align:middle line:84%
So now, what is b
of h given e star?

00:34:55.000 --> 00:35:10.340 align:middle line:90%


00:35:10.340 --> 00:35:13.380 align:middle line:84%
Well, if everyone choose
the same level of education,

00:35:13.380 --> 00:35:16.300 align:middle line:84%
their choice of education hasn't
revealed any new information

00:35:16.300 --> 00:35:17.250 align:middle line:90%
to me.

00:35:17.250 --> 00:35:19.000 align:middle line:84%
So my belief about
them should be the same

00:35:19.000 --> 00:35:21.460 align:middle line:84%
that it was, the
same as my prior.

00:35:21.460 --> 00:35:25.160 align:middle line:84%
And my prior belief was pi
that they were high ability.

00:35:25.160 --> 00:35:27.440 align:middle line:90%
So this must be pi.

00:35:27.440 --> 00:35:36.620 align:middle line:90%


00:35:36.620 --> 00:35:39.320 align:middle line:84%
Because-- I mean, you could
write out Bayes' rule,

00:35:39.320 --> 00:35:41.780 align:middle line:84%
but I think the basic
idea is if I thought

00:35:41.780 --> 00:35:44.300 align:middle line:84%
that the probability that
your high ability was pi,

00:35:44.300 --> 00:35:46.060 align:middle line:84%
and then I know that
whoever you are,

00:35:46.060 --> 00:35:48.300 align:middle line:84%
you're going to choose the
education level e star,

00:35:48.300 --> 00:35:49.758 align:middle line:84%
then the fact that
you chose e star

00:35:49.758 --> 00:35:52.300 align:middle line:84%
doesn't give me any new
information about your ability.

00:35:52.300 --> 00:35:54.880 align:middle line:90%
So it's-- this must still be pi.

00:35:54.880 --> 00:35:59.160 align:middle line:84%
And then again, we
have flexibility

00:35:59.160 --> 00:36:07.340 align:middle line:84%
is unconstrained
for all other e.

00:36:07.340 --> 00:36:10.080 align:middle line:90%


00:36:10.080 --> 00:36:12.920 align:middle line:84%
And part of
constructing equilibria

00:36:12.920 --> 00:36:17.160 align:middle line:84%
is going to be to think a bit
about what those beliefs have

00:36:17.160 --> 00:36:18.400 align:middle line:90%
to be.

00:36:18.400 --> 00:36:19.668 align:middle line:90%
So let's start with pooling.

00:36:19.668 --> 00:36:21.460 align:middle line:84%
I think pooling is
actually an easier case,

00:36:21.460 --> 00:36:22.700 align:middle line:90%
so let's start there.

00:36:22.700 --> 00:36:26.800 align:middle line:90%


00:36:26.800 --> 00:36:28.860 align:middle line:84%
So let's try to construct
our equilibrium.

00:36:28.860 --> 00:36:32.660 align:middle line:84%
So case 1 is the
pooling equilibrium.

00:36:32.660 --> 00:36:43.660 align:middle line:90%


00:36:43.660 --> 00:36:47.840 align:middle line:84%
And for the sender, we
know that they're choosing

00:36:47.840 --> 00:36:51.185 align:middle line:90%
el equals eh equals e star.

00:36:51.185 --> 00:36:53.560 align:middle line:84%
And we're going to try to
solve for this value of e star,

00:36:53.560 --> 00:36:56.460 align:middle line:84%
but we're just going to put it
in as a placeholder for now.

00:36:56.460 --> 00:37:00.580 align:middle line:84%
And now let's look
at the receiver.

00:37:00.580 --> 00:37:02.920 align:middle line:84%
The receiver needs
to choose w of e--

00:37:02.920 --> 00:37:08.820 align:middle line:90%


00:37:08.820 --> 00:37:11.480 align:middle line:84%
let's think through as the
analyst what we want to do here.

00:37:11.480 --> 00:37:15.460 align:middle line:84%
We're trying to construct
an equilibrium where

00:37:15.460 --> 00:37:17.340 align:middle line:84%
both the low type
and the high type

00:37:17.340 --> 00:37:20.380 align:middle line:84%
choose this education
level e star.

00:37:20.380 --> 00:37:24.180 align:middle line:84%
And now we're going to try to
choose what this wage is to make

00:37:24.180 --> 00:37:26.240 align:middle line:90%
e star optimal for the sender.

00:37:26.240 --> 00:37:29.420 align:middle line:84%
So any ideas about
what might be--

00:37:29.420 --> 00:37:33.260 align:middle line:84%
Well, let's think a little
bit about this wage.

00:37:33.260 --> 00:37:35.000 align:middle line:90%
Let's separate into two cases.

00:37:35.000 --> 00:37:41.220 align:middle line:90%


00:37:41.220 --> 00:37:44.320 align:middle line:90%
What is w of e star have to be?

00:37:44.320 --> 00:37:47.520 align:middle line:84%
We're going to argue that if
we're looking for an equilibrium

00:37:47.520 --> 00:37:51.520 align:middle line:84%
where both the low type and the
high type pool on this education

00:37:51.520 --> 00:37:53.320 align:middle line:84%
level e star, and
now we're going

00:37:53.320 --> 00:37:56.600 align:middle line:84%
to try to fill in, in this
equilibrium, what the receiver's

00:37:56.600 --> 00:37:57.813 align:middle line:90%
wage strategy is.

00:37:57.813 --> 00:37:59.480 align:middle line:84%
And I argue that here,
we basically know

00:37:59.480 --> 00:38:03.920 align:middle line:84%
what it has to be by the
combination of belief

00:38:03.920 --> 00:38:09.040 align:middle line:84%
consistency and
sequential optimality.

00:38:09.040 --> 00:38:10.940 align:middle line:84%
Well, we know what
the beliefs are here.

00:38:10.940 --> 00:38:13.120 align:middle line:84%
We just said the beliefs
are that your ability is

00:38:13.120 --> 00:38:15.200 align:middle line:90%
high probability pi.

00:38:15.200 --> 00:38:18.720 align:middle line:84%
And if those are our
beliefs, well, over--

00:38:18.720 --> 00:38:19.820 align:middle line:90%
where did we write it?

00:38:19.820 --> 00:38:22.340 align:middle line:84%
Here, we said if those
are your beliefs,

00:38:22.340 --> 00:38:24.560 align:middle line:84%
we computed what
the wage has to be.

00:38:24.560 --> 00:38:30.160 align:middle line:84%
So let's plug in pi for this
and 1 minus pi for this.

00:38:30.160 --> 00:38:34.720 align:middle line:84%
And indeed, we get
that this is h times pi

00:38:34.720 --> 00:38:37.860 align:middle line:90%
plus l times 1 minus pi.

00:38:37.860 --> 00:38:43.200 align:middle line:90%


00:38:43.200 --> 00:38:46.020 align:middle line:90%
So that's pinned down.

00:38:46.020 --> 00:38:48.380 align:middle line:84%
And I think this is a key
observation when analyzing

00:38:48.380 --> 00:38:49.620 align:middle line:90%
these signaling games.

00:38:49.620 --> 00:38:53.260 align:middle line:84%
The question is, I
said that your beliefs

00:38:53.260 --> 00:38:54.800 align:middle line:90%
off-path are unconstrained.

00:38:54.800 --> 00:38:57.340 align:middle line:84%
So if you observe-- when
the receiver observes

00:38:57.340 --> 00:38:59.443 align:middle line:84%
a level of education that's
different from e star,

00:38:59.443 --> 00:39:00.860 align:middle line:84%
we can fill in
whatever beliefs we

00:39:00.860 --> 00:39:03.660 align:middle line:84%
want for that kind of
receiver, but we can't

00:39:03.660 --> 00:39:05.580 align:middle line:90%
fill in whatever wage we want.

00:39:05.580 --> 00:39:07.940 align:middle line:84%
What is the range of
wages we could possibly

00:39:07.940 --> 00:39:11.820 align:middle line:84%
put in here that would be
consistent with receiver

00:39:11.820 --> 00:39:13.900 align:middle line:90%
optimality?

00:39:13.900 --> 00:39:16.620 align:middle line:84%
How high or how low
could these wages be?

00:39:16.620 --> 00:39:18.740 align:middle line:84%
Well, what's the most
pessimistic belief

00:39:18.740 --> 00:39:20.180 align:middle line:90%
the receiver could form?

00:39:20.180 --> 00:39:23.040 align:middle line:84%
What's the worst belief
they could have about the--

00:39:23.040 --> 00:39:25.100 align:middle line:84%
they could think you're
certainly a low type.

00:39:25.100 --> 00:39:26.660 align:middle line:84%
And if they think you're
certainly a low type, what

00:39:26.660 --> 00:39:28.280 align:middle line:90%
is the optimal wage to charge?

00:39:28.280 --> 00:39:31.220 align:middle line:90%


00:39:31.220 --> 00:39:32.700 align:middle line:90%
Just l.

00:39:32.700 --> 00:39:34.720 align:middle line:84%
And if they think you're
certainly a high type,

00:39:34.720 --> 00:39:36.620 align:middle line:90%
then the optimal wage is h.

00:39:36.620 --> 00:39:38.587 align:middle line:84%
And any belief
they form is going

00:39:38.587 --> 00:39:40.420 align:middle line:84%
to make it so that the
optimal wage for them

00:39:40.420 --> 00:39:42.420 align:middle line:84%
is going to be somewhere
between l and h.

00:39:42.420 --> 00:39:44.120 align:middle line:84%
It can't be lower
than l because there's

00:39:44.120 --> 00:39:46.480 align:middle line:84%
no belief that would make
the expectation lower than l.

00:39:46.480 --> 00:39:48.200 align:middle line:84%
It can't be higher
than h because there's

00:39:48.200 --> 00:39:50.617 align:middle line:84%
no belief that would make the
wage the optimal wage higher

00:39:50.617 --> 00:39:57.420 align:middle line:84%
than h, but we can actually
choose anything between l and h.

00:39:57.420 --> 00:40:01.120 align:middle line:90%


00:40:01.120 --> 00:40:03.260 align:middle line:84%
Because whatever choice
we make between l and h,

00:40:03.260 --> 00:40:05.000 align:middle line:84%
we can then construct
some belief that

00:40:05.000 --> 00:40:07.140 align:middle line:90%
justifies that wage choice.

00:40:07.140 --> 00:40:17.440 align:middle line:90%


00:40:17.440 --> 00:40:22.200 align:middle line:84%
So let's try to
think this through.

00:40:22.200 --> 00:40:26.800 align:middle line:84%
If you're trying to
construct a wage that makes--

00:40:26.800 --> 00:40:28.720 align:middle line:84%
we want to make
this an equilibrium.

00:40:28.720 --> 00:40:33.480 align:middle line:84%
So let's maybe think of
it graphing it down here.

00:40:33.480 --> 00:40:36.200 align:middle line:90%
Here's e star.

00:40:36.200 --> 00:40:37.500 align:middle line:90%
Here's our wage w.

00:40:37.500 --> 00:40:40.080 align:middle line:90%


00:40:40.080 --> 00:40:43.560 align:middle line:84%
The wage here is
pinned down to be this.

00:40:43.560 --> 00:40:45.120 align:middle line:84%
Maybe I'll give
notation for this.

00:40:45.120 --> 00:40:51.380 align:middle line:90%


00:40:51.380 --> 00:40:52.480 align:middle line:90%
I'll just write it down.

00:40:52.480 --> 00:40:54.200 align:middle line:90%
I'll just-- that's here.

00:40:54.200 --> 00:40:58.860 align:middle line:90%


00:40:58.860 --> 00:41:01.900 align:middle line:84%
And now the question
is, what choices of wage

00:41:01.900 --> 00:41:05.900 align:middle line:84%
should we make off-path to
ensure that the sender actually

00:41:05.900 --> 00:41:08.460 align:middle line:84%
wants to choose an
education level of e star?

00:41:08.460 --> 00:41:10.640 align:middle line:84%
So here, we're trying to
construct an equilibrium.

00:41:10.640 --> 00:41:14.220 align:middle line:84%
We don't want the sender to
have any profitable deviations.

00:41:14.220 --> 00:41:17.700 align:middle line:84%
So any ideas about what
wage we would set off-path

00:41:17.700 --> 00:41:21.540 align:middle line:84%
to encourage the sender to
choose this education level e

00:41:21.540 --> 00:41:22.040 align:middle line:90%
star?

00:41:22.040 --> 00:41:25.220 align:middle line:90%


00:41:25.220 --> 00:41:27.300 align:middle line:84%
Well, what's the
sender tempted to do?

00:41:27.300 --> 00:41:29.800 align:middle line:84%
They're supposed to choose
e star in equilibrium.

00:41:29.800 --> 00:41:32.157 align:middle line:84%
They're attempting to choose
a lower level of education

00:41:32.157 --> 00:41:34.240 align:middle line:84%
because if they choose a
lower level of education,

00:41:34.240 --> 00:41:35.980 align:middle line:90%
that's less costly for them.

00:41:35.980 --> 00:41:37.940 align:middle line:84%
So the only way to make
sure that that's not

00:41:37.940 --> 00:41:39.570 align:middle line:84%
a profitable deviation
is to say, well,

00:41:39.570 --> 00:41:41.210 align:middle line:84%
if you choose any
less education,

00:41:41.210 --> 00:41:43.230 align:middle line:84%
your wage is going
to drop a lot.

00:41:43.230 --> 00:41:44.710 align:middle line:84%
And let's look at
the extreme case

00:41:44.710 --> 00:41:47.890 align:middle line:84%
where the wage drops
all the way to l.

00:41:47.890 --> 00:41:55.422 align:middle line:90%


00:41:55.422 --> 00:41:57.630 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to think of
a wage function that says,

00:41:57.630 --> 00:41:59.950 align:middle line:84%
if you don't get enough
education e star,

00:41:59.950 --> 00:42:03.270 align:middle line:84%
you're going to get paid
a very low wage of l.

00:42:03.270 --> 00:42:06.270 align:middle line:84%
But if you go to college, if
you achieve this certification,

00:42:06.270 --> 00:42:07.770 align:middle line:84%
if you go to school
for long enough,

00:42:07.770 --> 00:42:11.990 align:middle line:84%
now you can get a job that
pays a higher wage of this.

00:42:11.990 --> 00:42:14.430 align:middle line:84%
And then what happens if e
is even higher than that?

00:42:14.430 --> 00:42:16.590 align:middle line:84%
Well, we want to make
sure that no one wants

00:42:16.590 --> 00:42:19.510 align:middle line:84%
to get even more education than
necessary, so we might as well

00:42:19.510 --> 00:42:20.730 align:middle line:90%
just keep the wage flat.

00:42:20.730 --> 00:42:24.590 align:middle line:90%


00:42:24.590 --> 00:42:26.590 align:middle line:84%
And if we keep the
wage flat, then we

00:42:26.590 --> 00:42:29.470 align:middle line:84%
know that, well, if someone can
get the same wage by choosing

00:42:29.470 --> 00:42:32.190 align:middle line:84%
e star as choosing a
higher level of education,

00:42:32.190 --> 00:42:34.630 align:middle line:84%
they would never pay more
to get an even higher level

00:42:34.630 --> 00:42:36.750 align:middle line:84%
of education if the wage
that they're going to get

00:42:36.750 --> 00:42:37.750 align:middle line:90%
is exactly the same.

00:42:37.750 --> 00:42:40.730 align:middle line:90%


00:42:40.730 --> 00:42:44.850 align:middle line:84%
So this is, I think,
a good conjecture

00:42:44.850 --> 00:42:47.430 align:middle line:84%
for what this
equilibrium might be.

00:42:47.430 --> 00:42:51.850 align:middle line:90%


00:42:51.850 --> 00:42:54.770 align:middle line:84%
So let's write this
out more formally.

00:42:54.770 --> 00:43:00.870 align:middle line:84%
What we have is w of e equals
l if e is less than e star.

00:43:00.870 --> 00:43:04.970 align:middle line:90%


00:43:04.970 --> 00:43:07.570 align:middle line:90%
Strictly less than e star.

00:43:07.570 --> 00:43:13.230 align:middle line:90%
And it's h pi plus l1 minus pi.

00:43:13.230 --> 00:43:25.650 align:middle line:90%


00:43:25.650 --> 00:43:32.030 align:middle line:84%
And then if you wanted
to fill in beliefs,

00:43:32.030 --> 00:43:42.510 align:middle line:84%
let's just be really precise,
our beliefs are-- well,

00:43:42.510 --> 00:43:46.030 align:middle line:84%
if e is less than e
star, the firm charges--

00:43:46.030 --> 00:43:47.817 align:middle line:90%
pays a wage of l.

00:43:47.817 --> 00:43:50.150 align:middle line:84%
The only way it's optimal for
them to charge a wage of l

00:43:50.150 --> 00:43:52.430 align:middle line:84%
is if they believe you're
certain to be a low type.

00:43:52.430 --> 00:43:54.210 align:middle line:90%
So we must have 0 here.

00:43:54.210 --> 00:44:00.670 align:middle line:90%


00:44:00.670 --> 00:44:02.390 align:middle line:84%
And then if they see
a level of education

00:44:02.390 --> 00:44:05.650 align:middle line:84%
that's at least e star, they're
paying exactly this wage.

00:44:05.650 --> 00:44:08.330 align:middle line:84%
So what must their belief be if
they see a level of education

00:44:08.330 --> 00:44:11.370 align:middle line:84%
at least e star to make that
wage optimal given their belief?

00:44:11.370 --> 00:44:14.150 align:middle line:90%


00:44:14.150 --> 00:44:16.430 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:44:16.430 --> 00:44:17.030 align:middle line:90%
Let's think.

00:44:17.030 --> 00:44:19.850 align:middle line:84%
If you're a high type,
what's the optimal wage?

00:44:19.850 --> 00:44:22.710 align:middle line:90%


00:44:22.710 --> 00:44:26.350 align:middle line:84%
If I know you're a high type,
now my optimal wage is h.

00:44:26.350 --> 00:44:30.950 align:middle line:84%
But the wage they're
charging is this,

00:44:30.950 --> 00:44:34.370 align:middle line:84%
which is h times pi
plus l times 1 minus pi.

00:44:34.370 --> 00:44:38.710 align:middle line:84%
So I need to find the belief
that makes my expected--

00:44:38.710 --> 00:44:43.490 align:middle line:84%
so that makes the expected
type equal to this.

00:44:43.490 --> 00:44:44.390 align:middle line:90%
Pi, exactly.

00:44:44.390 --> 00:44:47.127 align:middle line:90%


00:44:47.127 --> 00:44:49.210 align:middle line:84%
If I ever-- if there was
some education level that

00:44:49.210 --> 00:44:50.752 align:middle line:84%
made me believe you
were a high type,

00:44:50.752 --> 00:44:52.790 align:middle line:84%
then I'd want to charge
you a high wage there,

00:44:52.790 --> 00:44:53.910 align:middle line:84%
and now there might
be a deviation

00:44:53.910 --> 00:44:55.930 align:middle line:84%
because now someone would want
to get that education to prove

00:44:55.930 --> 00:44:56.972 align:middle line:90%
that they're a high type.

00:44:56.972 --> 00:44:59.450 align:middle line:84%
So let's think through
what's happening here.

00:44:59.450 --> 00:45:01.490 align:middle line:84%
The firms are
saying, look, there's

00:45:01.490 --> 00:45:05.050 align:middle line:84%
some minimal education level
e star that you need to get.

00:45:05.050 --> 00:45:06.965 align:middle line:84%
If you get less than
that education level,

00:45:06.965 --> 00:45:08.590 align:middle line:84%
I don't think you're
very high ability,

00:45:08.590 --> 00:45:10.382 align:middle line:84%
I think you're certain
to be a low ability,

00:45:10.382 --> 00:45:13.530 align:middle line:84%
and I'm going to charge
you a low wage, namely l.

00:45:13.530 --> 00:45:16.010 align:middle line:84%
But if you get enough
education-- let's

00:45:16.010 --> 00:45:18.090 align:middle line:84%
say you graduate from
college, then I'm

00:45:18.090 --> 00:45:20.230 align:middle line:84%
going to form a more
optimistic belief about you.

00:45:20.230 --> 00:45:23.170 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to believe that
you're just like anyone

00:45:23.170 --> 00:45:24.430 align:middle line:90%
from the population.

00:45:24.430 --> 00:45:26.490 align:middle line:84%
You're a high type
with probability pi

00:45:26.490 --> 00:45:28.730 align:middle line:84%
and you're low type with
probability 1 minus pi.

00:45:28.730 --> 00:45:31.910 align:middle line:84%
And because I believe that,
I'm going to charge you

00:45:31.910 --> 00:45:34.830 align:middle line:90%
a wage that's equal to this.

00:45:34.830 --> 00:45:37.190 align:middle line:84%
So we have this
two-tiered wage system

00:45:37.190 --> 00:45:39.715 align:middle line:84%
where if you graduate from
college, you get a wage premium,

00:45:39.715 --> 00:45:41.090 align:middle line:84%
but at least in
this equilibrium,

00:45:41.090 --> 00:45:43.530 align:middle line:84%
further education doesn't
further increase your wage.

00:45:43.530 --> 00:45:45.290 align:middle line:84%
And that's consistent
with some settings,

00:45:45.290 --> 00:45:47.090 align:middle line:84%
we could also construct
other equilibria.

00:45:47.090 --> 00:45:48.628 align:middle line:90%
But we're not quite done yet.

00:45:48.628 --> 00:45:50.670 align:middle line:84%
We're going to have to be
careful about the value

00:45:50.670 --> 00:45:54.270 align:middle line:84%
of e star to actually
make this in equilibrium.

00:45:54.270 --> 00:45:56.730 align:middle line:84%
So let's check-- let's
see what we've chosen,

00:45:56.730 --> 00:45:58.790 align:middle line:90%
what we've satisfied here.

00:45:58.790 --> 00:46:03.830 align:middle line:84%
Belief consistency is good
because the only time belief

00:46:03.830 --> 00:46:07.050 align:middle line:84%
consistency has any bite
here is when e equals e star.

00:46:07.050 --> 00:46:09.750 align:middle line:84%
That's the only on-path
level of education,

00:46:09.750 --> 00:46:11.350 align:middle line:84%
and we're consistent
with Bayes' rule,

00:46:11.350 --> 00:46:13.950 align:middle line:84%
so we're good with
belief consistency.

00:46:13.950 --> 00:46:16.550 align:middle line:84%
And by construction,
the receiver

00:46:16.550 --> 00:46:20.030 align:middle line:84%
is always paying the wage
that's equal to their updated

00:46:20.030 --> 00:46:21.950 align:middle line:84%
expectation of
the sender's type.

00:46:21.950 --> 00:46:24.110 align:middle line:90%
So we're good for the receiver.

00:46:24.110 --> 00:46:28.270 align:middle line:84%
The one thing we still have
to check is sender optimality.

00:46:28.270 --> 00:46:33.305 align:middle line:84%
So what could go wrong
in this equilibrium?

00:46:33.305 --> 00:46:34.930 align:middle line:84%
How could sender
optimality potentially

00:46:34.930 --> 00:46:37.270 align:middle line:84%
be violated if we
choose e star wrong?

00:46:37.270 --> 00:46:42.370 align:middle line:90%


00:46:42.370 --> 00:46:47.970 align:middle line:84%
Well, the problem is, if e
star is really, really big,

00:46:47.970 --> 00:46:51.850 align:middle line:84%
I might say, look, yes,
I get a higher wage

00:46:51.850 --> 00:46:54.150 align:middle line:84%
if I get at least e
star of education,

00:46:54.150 --> 00:46:56.450 align:middle line:84%
but that education
is so costly to me

00:46:56.450 --> 00:46:59.450 align:middle line:84%
that it's not worth me going to
school to get that higher wage.

00:46:59.450 --> 00:47:02.898 align:middle line:84%
So we need to check that e star
is intuitively-- e star is going

00:47:02.898 --> 00:47:04.690 align:middle line:84%
to have to be low enough
that it's actually

00:47:04.690 --> 00:47:06.610 align:middle line:84%
worthwhile for people
to go to college.

00:47:06.610 --> 00:47:08.730 align:middle line:84%
Just because there's a
college wage premium,

00:47:08.730 --> 00:47:10.330 align:middle line:84%
if the college wage
premium is smaller

00:47:10.330 --> 00:47:12.750 align:middle line:84%
than the cost of education,
people aren't going to go.

00:47:12.750 --> 00:47:14.690 align:middle line:84%
And in fact, if
we look over here,

00:47:14.690 --> 00:47:16.770 align:middle line:84%
the college wage
premium is not too far

00:47:16.770 --> 00:47:17.970 align:middle line:90%
from the cost of education.

00:47:17.970 --> 00:47:19.110 align:middle line:90%
So that is an issue.

00:47:19.110 --> 00:47:21.890 align:middle line:84%
We have to make sure that
the wage premium exceeds

00:47:21.890 --> 00:47:23.150 align:middle line:90%
the cost of education.

00:47:23.150 --> 00:47:25.110 align:middle line:84%
So we have a final
condition here.

00:47:25.110 --> 00:47:28.490 align:middle line:84%
So we need to-- it remains
to check sender optimality.

00:47:28.490 --> 00:47:37.390 align:middle line:90%


00:47:37.390 --> 00:47:38.650 align:middle line:90%
So at first, it's tricky.

00:47:38.650 --> 00:47:42.890 align:middle line:84%
We have to say, for both
types, t equals l and h.

00:47:42.890 --> 00:47:45.710 align:middle line:90%


00:47:45.710 --> 00:47:56.690 align:middle line:84%
Choosing e star is better than
any other education e in 0, 1.

00:47:56.690 --> 00:48:01.062 align:middle line:90%


00:48:01.062 --> 00:48:02.950 align:middle line:84%
Now this is a hard
condition because it

00:48:02.950 --> 00:48:04.790 align:middle line:90%
has to hold for both types.

00:48:04.790 --> 00:48:08.250 align:middle line:84%
And we're comparing e star to
any other level of education,

00:48:08.250 --> 00:48:10.590 align:middle line:84%
and there's infinitely many
other levels of education

00:48:10.590 --> 00:48:12.390 align:middle line:90%
someone might choose.

00:48:12.390 --> 00:48:13.990 align:middle line:84%
So the key to
simplifying things is

00:48:13.990 --> 00:48:15.910 align:middle line:84%
to say, well, instead
of saying it's

00:48:15.910 --> 00:48:18.630 align:middle line:84%
better than any other
education, let's try

00:48:18.630 --> 00:48:21.410 align:middle line:90%
to find the best deviation.

00:48:21.410 --> 00:48:25.030 align:middle line:90%


00:48:25.030 --> 00:48:26.698 align:middle line:84%
I could deviate from
e star to anything,

00:48:26.698 --> 00:48:28.240 align:middle line:84%
and we have to make
sure that I don't

00:48:28.240 --> 00:48:31.080 align:middle line:84%
want to deviate from e star to
any other level of education.

00:48:31.080 --> 00:48:33.840 align:middle line:84%
But some of these other
levels of education, it's

00:48:33.840 --> 00:48:35.600 align:middle line:84%
clear that I'm never
going to choose.

00:48:35.600 --> 00:48:38.515 align:middle line:84%
So let's focus on the
most attractive deviation,

00:48:38.515 --> 00:48:40.640 align:middle line:84%
and as long as the most
attractive deviation is not

00:48:40.640 --> 00:48:42.600 align:middle line:84%
profitable, then
no other deviation

00:48:42.600 --> 00:48:44.480 align:middle line:90%
can be profitable either.

00:48:44.480 --> 00:48:47.280 align:middle line:84%
So if I'm looking
at this wage chart,

00:48:47.280 --> 00:48:49.920 align:middle line:84%
this is the wage
function I'm facing,

00:48:49.920 --> 00:48:52.600 align:middle line:84%
and I'm choosing how
much education to get.

00:48:52.600 --> 00:48:54.480 align:middle line:90%
I'm already here at e star.

00:48:54.480 --> 00:48:56.313 align:middle line:84%
What would be the most
attractive deviation?

00:48:56.313 --> 00:48:58.063 align:middle line:84%
I'm not saying it's
profitable, but if I'm

00:48:58.063 --> 00:48:59.720 align:middle line:84%
going to deviate,
how should I deviate?

00:48:59.720 --> 00:49:02.420 align:middle line:84%
What's the best
possible deviation?

00:49:02.420 --> 00:49:04.240 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:49:04.240 --> 00:49:07.920 align:middle line:84%
0 is the only one that has
a chance, because clearly,

00:49:07.920 --> 00:49:10.640 align:middle line:84%
deviating above e star doesn't
make any sense because I'm

00:49:10.640 --> 00:49:14.880 align:middle line:84%
paying more in education and I'm
not getting any wage benefit.

00:49:14.880 --> 00:49:17.500 align:middle line:84%
So the only deviation
that could be profitable--

00:49:17.500 --> 00:49:19.560 align:middle line:84%
and I should put
"best" in parentheses.

00:49:19.560 --> 00:49:23.480 align:middle line:90%
It could be that-- yeah, anyway.

00:49:23.480 --> 00:49:27.100 align:middle line:84%
If I-- so I don't want to choose
any education higher than e

00:49:27.100 --> 00:49:27.740 align:middle line:90%
star.

00:49:27.740 --> 00:49:30.140 align:middle line:84%
If I choose education
strictly below e star,

00:49:30.140 --> 00:49:33.660 align:middle line:84%
well, my wage is the same no
matter what education I choose.

00:49:33.660 --> 00:49:35.800 align:middle line:84%
So since I hate school,
according to the model,

00:49:35.800 --> 00:49:37.800 align:middle line:84%
I might as well choose
the lowest possible level

00:49:37.800 --> 00:49:38.360 align:middle line:90%
of education.

00:49:38.360 --> 00:49:39.900 align:middle line:84%
So the only thing
we have to check

00:49:39.900 --> 00:49:43.220 align:middle line:90%
is a deviation of e equals 0.

00:49:43.220 --> 00:49:45.780 align:middle line:90%


00:49:45.780 --> 00:49:49.020 align:middle line:84%
And now we have to check
that both for the high type

00:49:49.020 --> 00:49:50.120 align:middle line:90%
and for the low type.

00:49:50.120 --> 00:49:52.820 align:middle line:84%
So let's write down the
two conditions we need.

00:49:52.820 --> 00:49:56.700 align:middle line:84%
So let's look at t
equals l and t equals h.

00:49:56.700 --> 00:49:59.580 align:middle line:84%
We want to compare what
utility that person get

00:49:59.580 --> 00:50:01.940 align:middle line:90%
if they choose e star versus 0.

00:50:01.940 --> 00:50:06.380 align:middle line:84%
So if they choose e star,
their utility is going to be--

00:50:06.380 --> 00:50:10.260 align:middle line:90%
I really need notation for this.

00:50:10.260 --> 00:50:13.060 align:middle line:90%
Let me call this prior mean.

00:50:13.060 --> 00:50:17.720 align:middle line:84%
Let me call this
mu, mu for mean.

00:50:17.720 --> 00:50:20.220 align:middle line:84%
So let me use--
just call that mu.

00:50:20.220 --> 00:50:25.840 align:middle line:84%
So if I choose e star, I
get a wage of mu minus 1

00:50:25.840 --> 00:50:31.080 align:middle line:90%
over l e star squared.

00:50:31.080 --> 00:50:36.700 align:middle line:90%
And that must be better than l.

00:50:36.700 --> 00:50:39.400 align:middle line:90%


00:50:39.400 --> 00:50:42.620 align:middle line:84%
Maybe, just so we see
what's going on here.

00:50:42.620 --> 00:50:45.280 align:middle line:90%


00:50:45.280 --> 00:50:47.680 align:middle line:84%
OK, this is the key condition,
let's go over it slowly.

00:50:47.680 --> 00:50:49.240 align:middle line:90%
I'm a type l.

00:50:49.240 --> 00:50:53.080 align:middle line:84%
If I do what I'm supposed to
do, I get paid a wage of mu.

00:50:53.080 --> 00:50:55.520 align:middle line:84%
I choose a level of
education e star.

00:50:55.520 --> 00:50:59.220 align:middle line:84%
My cost of that education is
1 over l times e star squared.

00:50:59.220 --> 00:51:02.880 align:middle line:84%
That comes from the formula
for my utility up here.

00:51:02.880 --> 00:51:05.520 align:middle line:84%
And we've plugged
in for my wage mu

00:51:05.520 --> 00:51:08.260 align:middle line:84%
because that's the wage
I get if I choose e star.

00:51:08.260 --> 00:51:09.960 align:middle line:90%
So let's remind ourselves here.

00:51:09.960 --> 00:51:13.720 align:middle line:90%
This is w of e star.

00:51:13.720 --> 00:51:16.000 align:middle line:84%
If instead, I choose
zero education,

00:51:16.000 --> 00:51:20.440 align:middle line:84%
well, my wage is l
because we see over here--

00:51:20.440 --> 00:51:23.180 align:middle line:84%
and maybe I'll write
that down here.

00:51:23.180 --> 00:51:25.340 align:middle line:90%
This is w of 0.

00:51:25.340 --> 00:51:28.860 align:middle line:84%
This is the wage that's offered
to me if I choose no education.

00:51:28.860 --> 00:51:31.460 align:middle line:84%
And if I choose no
education, this is the cost,

00:51:31.460 --> 00:51:34.280 align:middle line:84%
but this cost is just 0, because
if I choose no education,

00:51:34.280 --> 00:51:35.162 align:middle line:90%
the cost is 0.

00:51:35.162 --> 00:51:37.120 align:middle line:84%
So we don't even have to
worry about this term.

00:51:37.120 --> 00:51:39.400 align:middle line:84%
0 squared is 0, I can
just cross this out.

00:51:39.400 --> 00:51:41.980 align:middle line:90%


00:51:41.980 --> 00:51:43.820 align:middle line:84%
And now let's go
down to l equals h

00:51:43.820 --> 00:51:45.240 align:middle line:90%
and write down the same formula.

00:51:45.240 --> 00:51:54.195 align:middle line:90%


00:51:54.195 --> 00:51:55.820 align:middle line:84%
Now I think a lot of
people are tempted

00:51:55.820 --> 00:51:59.040 align:middle line:84%
to put an h on the right-hand
side, but that's not correct.

00:51:59.040 --> 00:52:01.060 align:middle line:84%
Because remember, what
is l representing?

00:52:01.060 --> 00:52:02.960 align:middle line:90%
l is the wage the firm charges.

00:52:02.960 --> 00:52:04.820 align:middle line:90%
If you get no education.

00:52:04.820 --> 00:52:06.860 align:middle line:84%
If I deviate and
get no education,

00:52:06.860 --> 00:52:10.180 align:middle line:84%
I'm offered a wage of l whether
I'm a high type or low type.

00:52:10.180 --> 00:52:12.440 align:middle line:90%
So this is coming from w of 0.

00:52:12.440 --> 00:52:14.140 align:middle line:84%
It's not about my
true type, it's

00:52:14.140 --> 00:52:15.960 align:middle line:84%
about what the firm
is actually doing.

00:52:15.960 --> 00:52:23.000 align:middle line:90%


00:52:23.000 --> 00:52:30.320 align:middle line:84%
So here are the two
conditions we have,

00:52:30.320 --> 00:52:33.320 align:middle line:84%
and we need both
of them to hold.

00:52:33.320 --> 00:52:34.880 align:middle line:84%
It turns out, one
of these conditions

00:52:34.880 --> 00:52:38.700 align:middle line:84%
is going to be more
demanding than the other.

00:52:38.700 --> 00:52:41.340 align:middle line:84%
Which of these is
harder to satisfy?

00:52:41.340 --> 00:52:46.098 align:middle line:90%


00:52:46.098 --> 00:52:47.140 align:middle line:90%
We have two inequalities.

00:52:47.140 --> 00:52:47.838 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:52:47.838 --> 00:52:48.880 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: The one on top.

00:52:48.880 --> 00:52:49.060 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: OK.

00:52:49.060 --> 00:52:49.880 align:middle line:90%
And why?

00:52:49.880 --> 00:52:51.400 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Because
the 1 over l times

00:52:51.400 --> 00:52:54.480 align:middle line:84%
e star squared term is greater
than the 1 over h times

00:52:54.480 --> 00:52:55.500 align:middle line:90%
the e star squared term.

00:52:55.500 --> 00:52:56.500 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Example right.

00:52:56.500 --> 00:52:59.338 align:middle line:84%
So because l is smaller,
1 over l is bigger.

00:52:59.338 --> 00:53:00.880 align:middle line:84%
So we're subtracting
a bigger number,

00:53:00.880 --> 00:53:02.145 align:middle line:90%
and that's harder to satisfy.

00:53:02.145 --> 00:53:03.020 align:middle line:90%
And this makes sense.

00:53:03.020 --> 00:53:05.800 align:middle line:84%
Let's think about what each
of these inequalities say.

00:53:05.800 --> 00:53:07.960 align:middle line:84%
If we move these things
around, what we're saying

00:53:07.960 --> 00:53:11.160 align:middle line:84%
is, let's think of moving
the cost to this side

00:53:11.160 --> 00:53:13.040 align:middle line:90%
and subtracting l on this side.

00:53:13.040 --> 00:53:14.700 align:middle line:90%
Let me write that down here.

00:53:14.700 --> 00:53:16.140 align:middle line:90%
This is the key condition.

00:53:16.140 --> 00:53:20.260 align:middle line:84%
So the key condition
is mu minus l

00:53:20.260 --> 00:53:24.960 align:middle line:84%
is greater than or equal
to 1 over l e star squared.

00:53:24.960 --> 00:53:27.780 align:middle line:84%
And this, I think, is a
very natural interpretation.

00:53:27.780 --> 00:53:31.540 align:middle line:84%
Mu minus l is the, quote
unquote, "college wage premium."

00:53:31.540 --> 00:53:34.420 align:middle line:84%
This is the difference in wage
I get if I choose an education

00:53:34.420 --> 00:53:37.320 align:middle line:84%
level of e star versus
education level of 0

00:53:37.320 --> 00:53:42.260 align:middle line:84%
because if I choose e star, I
get mu; if I choose 0, I get l.

00:53:42.260 --> 00:53:43.820 align:middle line:90%
So this is the wage premium.

00:53:43.820 --> 00:53:46.740 align:middle line:84%
And this condition says
the wage premium is higher

00:53:46.740 --> 00:53:48.300 align:middle line:90%
than the cost of education.

00:53:48.300 --> 00:53:50.980 align:middle line:84%
And indeed, it's worth it
for me to go to college

00:53:50.980 --> 00:53:53.580 align:middle line:84%
if the wage premium I get
from going to college-- that

00:53:53.580 --> 00:53:55.860 align:middle line:84%
is, the additional wage I
get from going to college

00:53:55.860 --> 00:53:57.620 align:middle line:84%
more than outweighs
the cost I have

00:53:57.620 --> 00:53:59.220 align:middle line:90%
to bear of going to college.

00:53:59.220 --> 00:54:03.340 align:middle line:84%
Now, we also need to check
that the wage premium exceeds

00:54:03.340 --> 00:54:05.500 align:middle line:84%
the cost of education
for the high type.

00:54:05.500 --> 00:54:08.860 align:middle line:84%
But for the high type, going
to college is less costly.

00:54:08.860 --> 00:54:10.940 align:middle line:84%
So as long as we check
that the wage premium is

00:54:10.940 --> 00:54:12.510 align:middle line:84%
worth it for the
low type, well, it's

00:54:12.510 --> 00:54:14.510 align:middle line:84%
certainly going to be
worth it for the high type

00:54:14.510 --> 00:54:16.290 align:middle line:84%
because they get the
same wage premium,

00:54:16.290 --> 00:54:17.850 align:middle line:84%
but they find
education less costly,

00:54:17.850 --> 00:54:19.390 align:middle line:84%
and that's exactly
the observation

00:54:19.390 --> 00:54:20.550 align:middle line:90%
that was made at the end.

00:54:20.550 --> 00:54:22.970 align:middle line:84%
So this is our
equilibrium condition.

00:54:22.970 --> 00:54:25.052 align:middle line:84%
And as long as
this is satisfied,

00:54:25.052 --> 00:54:27.010 align:middle line:84%
then this is indeed going
to be an equilibrium.

00:54:27.010 --> 00:54:31.310 align:middle line:90%


00:54:31.310 --> 00:54:34.230 align:middle line:84%
So we actually have constructed
many different equilibria

00:54:34.230 --> 00:54:36.172 align:middle line:84%
parametrized by
this level e star.

00:54:36.172 --> 00:54:37.630 align:middle line:84%
There could be an
equilibrium where

00:54:37.630 --> 00:54:40.528 align:middle line:84%
we say if you go to school
for 0.3-- for e equals 0.3,

00:54:40.528 --> 00:54:41.570 align:middle line:90%
then you get a high wage.

00:54:41.570 --> 00:54:42.510 align:middle line:84%
There could also
be an equilibrium

00:54:42.510 --> 00:54:44.802 align:middle line:84%
where if you go to school
for 0.4, you get a high wage.

00:54:44.802 --> 00:54:46.552 align:middle line:84%
And these are both
conceivable equilibria,

00:54:46.552 --> 00:54:48.135 align:middle line:84%
and there's a huge
range of equilibria

00:54:48.135 --> 00:54:49.270 align:middle line:90%
that we can construct here.

00:54:49.270 --> 00:54:50.890 align:middle line:90%
Any questions on this?

00:54:50.890 --> 00:54:55.150 align:middle line:90%


00:54:55.150 --> 00:54:59.030 align:middle line:90%
OK, so now, let's look for a--

00:54:59.030 --> 00:55:00.730 align:middle line:84%
let's look for a
separating equilibria.

00:55:00.730 --> 00:55:02.250 align:middle line:84%
So, so far, we've
just done pooling.

00:55:02.250 --> 00:55:05.710 align:middle line:90%


00:55:05.710 --> 00:55:08.250 align:middle line:84%
Let's look for a
separating equilibria.

00:55:08.250 --> 00:55:24.250 align:middle line:90%


00:55:24.250 --> 00:55:26.790 align:middle line:84%
Now let's look for a
separating equilibria.

00:55:26.790 --> 00:55:34.930 align:middle line:90%


00:55:34.930 --> 00:55:40.970 align:middle line:84%
So what that means is we
have el is not equal to eh.

00:55:40.970 --> 00:55:43.050 align:middle line:84%
So instead of just
solving for e star

00:55:43.050 --> 00:55:46.030 align:middle line:90%
we have to solve for el and eh.

00:55:46.030 --> 00:55:48.610 align:middle line:90%


00:55:48.610 --> 00:55:54.270 align:middle line:84%
And now, let's try to think of
what w is going to have to be.

00:55:54.270 --> 00:56:07.507 align:middle line:90%


00:56:07.507 --> 00:56:09.090 align:middle line:84%
So I think on a
problem set, if you're

00:56:09.090 --> 00:56:11.550 align:middle line:84%
trying to construct equilibrium,
it's always very helpful

00:56:11.550 --> 00:56:15.750 align:middle line:84%
to separate what the firm-- or
what the receiver does on-path

00:56:15.750 --> 00:56:17.870 align:middle line:84%
from what the receiver
does off-path.

00:56:17.870 --> 00:56:20.190 align:middle line:84%
Because on-path,
Bayes' rule applies,

00:56:20.190 --> 00:56:22.270 align:middle line:84%
and we really have
precise control

00:56:22.270 --> 00:56:24.230 align:middle line:90%
over what the wage has to be.

00:56:24.230 --> 00:56:26.350 align:middle line:84%
Off-path, Bayes rule
can't be applied

00:56:26.350 --> 00:56:28.830 align:middle line:84%
because we divide by 0
when we apply Bayes' rule,

00:56:28.830 --> 00:56:32.870 align:middle line:84%
and we have a lot of flexibility
in what this wage is.

00:56:32.870 --> 00:56:34.750 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:56:34.750 --> 00:56:37.050 align:middle line:84%
So if we're looking
for an equilibrium,

00:56:37.050 --> 00:56:39.750 align:middle line:84%
a separating equilibrium, with
these two levels of education,

00:56:39.750 --> 00:56:42.310 align:middle line:84%
eh and el, what
is the firm's wage

00:56:42.310 --> 00:56:46.090 align:middle line:84%
going to have to be if they
see an education level of el?

00:56:46.090 --> 00:56:47.590 align:middle line:84%
Or maybe the first
question is, what

00:56:47.590 --> 00:56:48.890 align:middle line:84%
is their belief
going to have to be,

00:56:48.890 --> 00:56:50.890 align:middle line:84%
and then what is their
wage going to have to be?

00:56:50.890 --> 00:56:57.270 align:middle line:90%


00:56:57.270 --> 00:56:59.150 align:middle line:84%
Well, what do they learn
if they see someone

00:56:59.150 --> 00:57:01.910 align:middle line:90%
choose education level el?

00:57:01.910 --> 00:57:05.630 align:middle line:84%
They learn that they must be a
low type because the high types

00:57:05.630 --> 00:57:06.827 align:middle line:90%
always choose eh.

00:57:06.827 --> 00:57:09.410 align:middle line:84%
Notice, this is a key difference
with the pooling equilibrium.

00:57:09.410 --> 00:57:11.950 align:middle line:84%
If all the types choose the
same level of education,

00:57:11.950 --> 00:57:14.810 align:middle line:84%
I don't learn about your type
by seeing your education level.

00:57:14.810 --> 00:57:17.610 align:middle line:84%
But if different types choose
different levels of education,

00:57:17.610 --> 00:57:19.890 align:middle line:84%
now, your choice of
education reveals

00:57:19.890 --> 00:57:22.050 align:middle line:84%
a lot of information
to me as the receiver.

00:57:22.050 --> 00:57:24.850 align:middle line:84%
So when I el, I know
that you're a low type

00:57:24.850 --> 00:57:27.050 align:middle line:84%
and I must set the
wage equal to l.

00:57:27.050 --> 00:57:33.070 align:middle line:84%
And maybe in parentheses,
this is because eh of el is 0.

00:57:33.070 --> 00:57:35.690 align:middle line:90%


00:57:35.690 --> 00:57:37.950 align:middle line:84%
What about if I see an
education level of eh?

00:57:37.950 --> 00:57:38.990 align:middle line:90%
What do I conclude?

00:57:38.990 --> 00:57:42.050 align:middle line:90%


00:57:42.050 --> 00:57:42.610 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:57:42.610 --> 00:57:43.550 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: I want to say type h.

00:57:43.550 --> 00:57:44.758 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: You must be type h.

00:57:44.758 --> 00:57:46.410 align:middle line:90%
So now this is h.

00:57:46.410 --> 00:57:52.310 align:middle line:84%
And now we have b of
h given eh equals 1.

00:57:52.310 --> 00:57:55.690 align:middle line:90%


00:57:55.690 --> 00:58:00.450 align:middle line:84%
And now the off-path wage,
there's kind of a trick

00:58:00.450 --> 00:58:03.290 align:middle line:90%
that we sometimes can use.

00:58:03.290 --> 00:58:05.510 align:middle line:84%
If we really want to
make this an equilibrium,

00:58:05.510 --> 00:58:06.990 align:middle line:84%
we want to make
it as unappealing

00:58:06.990 --> 00:58:10.590 align:middle line:84%
as possible to deviate to any
off-path level of education.

00:58:10.590 --> 00:58:12.570 align:middle line:84%
So how could-- if we
have flexibility here

00:58:12.570 --> 00:58:14.230 align:middle line:84%
to choose this
wage, how could we

00:58:14.230 --> 00:58:15.950 align:middle line:84%
choose this wage
to make deviations

00:58:15.950 --> 00:58:18.412 align:middle line:90%
unappealing for the sender?

00:58:18.412 --> 00:58:20.370 align:middle line:84%
And this may be the most
realistic equilibrium,

00:58:20.370 --> 00:58:23.710 align:middle line:84%
but it's kind of a good trick
for computing equilibria.

00:58:23.710 --> 00:58:27.490 align:middle line:84%
So 0 would really deter
deviations, but we can't do 0.

00:58:27.490 --> 00:58:29.622 align:middle line:84%
Why can't we necessarily--
why can't we do 0?

00:58:29.622 --> 00:58:31.330 align:middle line:84%
That would violate
one of our conditions.

00:58:31.330 --> 00:58:33.040 align:middle line:84%
The problem is, it
would violate r,

00:58:33.040 --> 00:58:35.290 align:middle line:84%
and you may not have been
here when we went over this,

00:58:35.290 --> 00:58:37.150 align:middle line:84%
but this would
violate r, and I think

00:58:37.150 --> 00:58:39.890 align:middle line:84%
I just erased it, our
receiver optimality condition.

00:58:39.890 --> 00:58:45.670 align:middle line:84%
So off-path, Bayes' rule
doesn't impose any constraints

00:58:45.670 --> 00:58:46.610 align:middle line:90%
in our belief.

00:58:46.610 --> 00:58:48.230 align:middle line:84%
But whatever our
belief is, we still

00:58:48.230 --> 00:58:50.430 align:middle line:84%
have to be behaving
optimally given our belief.

00:58:50.430 --> 00:58:52.933 align:middle line:84%
And the worst possible belief
we could form off-path, worse

00:58:52.933 --> 00:58:54.350 align:middle line:84%
from the standpoint
of the sender,

00:58:54.350 --> 00:58:56.910 align:middle line:84%
would to be certain
that you're a low type.

00:58:56.910 --> 00:58:59.630 align:middle line:84%
So if we're certain you're a low
type, the wage we would charge

00:58:59.630 --> 00:59:00.590 align:middle line:90%
is l.

00:59:00.590 --> 00:59:04.690 align:middle line:84%
So you're right that a wage
of 0 would be a stronger

00:59:04.690 --> 00:59:07.070 align:middle line:84%
punishment for the
sender for deviating,

00:59:07.070 --> 00:59:08.790 align:middle line:84%
but it's not a
credible punishment

00:59:08.790 --> 00:59:11.130 align:middle line:84%
because even if the firm
thinks you're always

00:59:11.130 --> 00:59:13.130 align:middle line:84%
a low-ability person,
they'd rather charge you

00:59:13.130 --> 00:59:15.710 align:middle line:84%
a wage of l, which is strictly
positive than a wage of 0,

00:59:15.710 --> 00:59:20.010 align:middle line:90%
but that's a helpful comment.

00:59:20.010 --> 00:59:21.090 align:middle line:90%
So here we are.

00:59:21.090 --> 00:59:27.090 align:middle line:84%
Now I think we have some trouble
because if I'm a low type, well,

00:59:27.090 --> 00:59:31.170 align:middle line:84%
why should I choose el and
get l if I could just-- well,

00:59:31.170 --> 00:59:33.990 align:middle line:84%
any other education level I
choose, I'll get l as well,

00:59:33.990 --> 00:59:37.290 align:middle line:84%
so why don't I just deviate
to education level equals 0?

00:59:37.290 --> 00:59:39.530 align:middle line:84%
If I'm the low type,
I think deviating

00:59:39.530 --> 00:59:42.810 align:middle line:90%
to equals 0 looks pretty good.

00:59:42.810 --> 00:59:45.490 align:middle line:84%
So in fact, we immediately
see a strong constraint

00:59:45.490 --> 00:59:47.550 align:middle line:84%
on what happens in a
separating equilibrium.

00:59:47.550 --> 00:59:55.490 align:middle line:84%
So first observation,
note, must be 0.

00:59:55.490 --> 00:59:58.450 align:middle line:84%
If el was not 0,
then the low type

00:59:58.450 --> 01:00:01.270 align:middle line:84%
would deviate to choose
an education level of 0.

01:00:01.270 --> 01:00:03.470 align:middle line:84%
And let's think
through why this is.

01:00:03.470 --> 01:00:06.270 align:middle line:84%
In a separating
equilibrium, put yourself

01:00:06.270 --> 01:00:07.830 align:middle line:90%
in the position of a low type.

01:00:07.830 --> 01:00:10.830 align:middle line:84%
When you follow your equilibrium
strategy and choose el,

01:00:10.830 --> 01:00:14.310 align:middle line:84%
you're fully revealing to the
firm that you're a low ability

01:00:14.310 --> 01:00:17.030 align:middle line:84%
or that you're a low
type sender, which

01:00:17.030 --> 01:00:19.630 align:middle line:84%
is the worst possible thing
they can believe about you.

01:00:19.630 --> 01:00:23.470 align:middle line:84%
So it never makes sense to pay
a positive cost to go to school,

01:00:23.470 --> 01:00:26.065 align:middle line:84%
only to be believed that
you're the worst possible type.

01:00:26.065 --> 01:00:28.190 align:middle line:84%
So if I'm going to induce
the worst possible belief

01:00:28.190 --> 01:00:30.310 align:middle line:84%
about myself, I might
as well pay no cost

01:00:30.310 --> 01:00:33.550 align:middle line:90%
and not go to school at all.

01:00:33.550 --> 01:00:36.630 align:middle line:90%
So now el equals 0.

01:00:36.630 --> 01:00:37.150 align:middle line:90%
Good.

01:00:37.150 --> 01:00:39.830 align:middle line:90%


01:00:39.830 --> 01:00:42.930 align:middle line:90%
And let's try to graph this.

01:00:42.930 --> 01:00:45.328 align:middle line:90%


01:00:45.328 --> 01:00:46.870 align:middle line:84%
Let's do our graph
again, and I think

01:00:46.870 --> 01:00:51.390 align:middle line:84%
it's actually helpful to try to
represent the sender optimality

01:00:51.390 --> 01:00:53.510 align:middle line:84%
conditions on the graph,
so let's try to do this.

01:00:53.510 --> 01:00:56.390 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to
look at a wage here.

01:00:56.390 --> 01:00:58.310 align:middle line:90%
el is going to be 0.

01:00:58.310 --> 01:01:02.200 align:middle line:84%
eh is not equal to el, so eh
has to be strictly positive.

01:01:02.200 --> 01:01:06.920 align:middle line:84%
So let's just put a
guess of where eh is.

01:01:06.920 --> 01:01:09.640 align:middle line:84%
And we said that
the wage is going

01:01:09.640 --> 01:01:13.440 align:middle line:84%
to be l If you deviate-- if
you do something off-path.

01:01:13.440 --> 01:01:17.240 align:middle line:90%
So here's l.

01:01:17.240 --> 01:01:18.840 align:middle line:90%
Here's h.

01:01:18.840 --> 01:01:28.720 align:middle line:84%
And to here, the wage is
going to look like this.

01:01:28.720 --> 01:01:33.280 align:middle line:90%
Now, at eh, the wage jumps to h.

01:01:33.280 --> 01:01:40.160 align:middle line:84%
So this is an open circle,
this is a closed circle here,

01:01:40.160 --> 01:01:42.000 align:middle line:84%
and then the wage
continues down.

01:01:42.000 --> 01:01:46.680 align:middle line:84%
So this is, if we want to be
really safe, what we would do.

01:01:46.680 --> 01:01:49.120 align:middle line:84%
But to be a little
more realistic,

01:01:49.120 --> 01:01:52.400 align:middle line:84%
well, we want to make
sure that no one wants

01:01:52.400 --> 01:01:54.540 align:middle line:84%
to deviate to this high
level of education.

01:01:54.540 --> 01:01:56.640 align:middle line:90%
But this seems like--

01:01:56.640 --> 01:01:58.100 align:middle line:84%
I mean, it could
be an equilibrium,

01:01:58.100 --> 01:01:59.660 align:middle line:84%
but it seems kind
of weird to say,

01:01:59.660 --> 01:02:01.160 align:middle line:84%
if you get a really
high education,

01:02:01.160 --> 01:02:02.600 align:middle line:90%
we believe you're a low type.

01:02:02.600 --> 01:02:04.725 align:middle line:84%
I mean, it's possible, but
it seems a little weird.

01:02:04.725 --> 01:02:06.980 align:middle line:84%
So I think often
what we'll do is

01:02:06.980 --> 01:02:10.160 align:middle line:84%
we'll choose the off-path
beliefs like this

01:02:10.160 --> 01:02:13.780 align:middle line:84%
so that actually, if you choose
any education level above eh,

01:02:13.780 --> 01:02:15.780 align:middle line:90%
we'll still pay you a wage of h.

01:02:15.780 --> 01:02:17.300 align:middle line:90%
Now why is that OK?

01:02:17.300 --> 01:02:20.900 align:middle line:84%
Well, if you can always get
an education level of eh

01:02:20.900 --> 01:02:22.740 align:middle line:84%
and get a wage of
h, no one's going

01:02:22.740 --> 01:02:25.157 align:middle line:84%
to ever want to deviate
beyond eh to get the same wage

01:02:25.157 --> 01:02:26.240 align:middle line:90%
that they would get at eh.

01:02:26.240 --> 01:02:29.280 align:middle line:84%
So that going off your
comment, to be really safe,

01:02:29.280 --> 01:02:32.300 align:middle line:84%
we'd set we to bl, but
really, the important thing

01:02:32.300 --> 01:02:33.517 align:middle line:90%
is that it's l down here.

01:02:33.517 --> 01:02:36.100 align:middle line:84%
Beyond here, we can make it h
and we're not going to introduce

01:02:36.100 --> 01:02:38.500 align:middle line:90%
any deviations, so let's--

01:02:38.500 --> 01:02:44.420 align:middle line:84%
bl for e less than eh, that's
kind of the important thing.

01:02:44.420 --> 01:02:46.920 align:middle line:84%
And let's try to
do it like this.

01:02:46.920 --> 01:02:50.900 align:middle line:90%


01:02:50.900 --> 01:02:53.340 align:middle line:84%
So notice that this graph
looks a lot like what

01:02:53.340 --> 01:02:55.000 align:middle line:84%
we had in the
pooling equilibrium,

01:02:55.000 --> 01:02:56.208 align:middle line:90%
but there's a key difference.

01:02:56.208 --> 01:03:00.640 align:middle line:84%
So like the pooling equilibrium,
if your education was too low,

01:03:00.640 --> 01:03:04.060 align:middle line:84%
we paid you l, but in
the pooling equilibrium,

01:03:04.060 --> 01:03:06.060 align:middle line:84%
if you got to a high
enough level of education,

01:03:06.060 --> 01:03:08.860 align:middle line:84%
we paid you the average
ability in the population.

01:03:08.860 --> 01:03:10.440 align:middle line:90%
Now we pay you a lot more.

01:03:10.440 --> 01:03:11.880 align:middle line:90%
We pay you h.

01:03:11.880 --> 01:03:14.500 align:middle line:84%
Because now, if I see a
high level of education,

01:03:14.500 --> 01:03:15.743 align:middle line:90%
I draw a different inference.

01:03:15.743 --> 01:03:17.160 align:middle line:84%
As the receiver,
I say, oh, if you

01:03:17.160 --> 01:03:20.960 align:middle line:84%
got a high level of education,
you must be a high type person.

01:03:20.960 --> 01:03:22.820 align:middle line:84%
So this wage is
much, much higher,

01:03:22.820 --> 01:03:24.580 align:middle line:84%
and we'll find that,
not too surprisingly,

01:03:24.580 --> 01:03:26.122 align:middle line:84%
eh is going to have
to be pretty high

01:03:26.122 --> 01:03:29.280 align:middle line:84%
as well to ensure that
no one wants to deviate.

01:03:29.280 --> 01:03:29.780 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:03:29.780 --> 01:03:33.240 align:middle line:84%
So we have our wage
function written down.

01:03:33.240 --> 01:03:37.840 align:middle line:84%
The wage function satisfies
receiver optimality

01:03:37.840 --> 01:03:39.400 align:middle line:90%
by our construction.

01:03:39.400 --> 01:03:43.080 align:middle line:90%
Let's just check in words.

01:03:43.080 --> 01:03:45.920 align:middle line:84%
Bayes rule applies only
at these two points

01:03:45.920 --> 01:03:48.200 align:middle line:84%
because at this
point, at e equals 0,

01:03:48.200 --> 01:03:51.040 align:middle line:84%
that's the level of education
chosen by the low type.

01:03:51.040 --> 01:03:52.640 align:middle line:84%
We believe that
you're the low type,

01:03:52.640 --> 01:03:54.340 align:middle line:90%
and we charge you a wage of l.

01:03:54.340 --> 01:03:55.540 align:middle line:90%
That's great.

01:03:55.540 --> 01:03:59.135 align:middle line:84%
Here, eh, If we see that, we
believe you're a high type,

01:03:59.135 --> 01:04:01.260 align:middle line:84%
and we charge you that--
we pay you the wage that's

01:04:01.260 --> 01:04:02.640 align:middle line:90%
optimal given that belief.

01:04:02.640 --> 01:04:04.700 align:middle line:84%
So Bayes' rule
pins us down here.

01:04:04.700 --> 01:04:06.890 align:middle line:84%
Everything outside
of that is off-path,

01:04:06.890 --> 01:04:08.640 align:middle line:84%
we had a lot of
flexibility in our choice,

01:04:08.640 --> 01:04:10.820 align:middle line:84%
and we made those choices
to try to deter deviations

01:04:10.820 --> 01:04:12.700 align:middle line:90%
by the sender.

01:04:12.700 --> 01:04:13.200 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:04:13.200 --> 01:04:16.340 align:middle line:84%
So now, there's two
things we have to check.

01:04:16.340 --> 01:04:22.340 align:middle line:84%
We have to check that the
low type-- so maybe I'll

01:04:22.340 --> 01:04:23.320 align:middle line:90%
just put it over here.

01:04:23.320 --> 01:04:26.980 align:middle line:90%


01:04:26.980 --> 01:04:36.220 align:middle line:84%
The low type prefers el
to all other e, e prime,

01:04:36.220 --> 01:04:39.800 align:middle line:90%
and the high type prefers eh.

01:04:39.800 --> 01:04:44.900 align:middle line:90%


01:04:44.900 --> 01:04:48.100 align:middle line:84%
So this is what sender
optimality means.

01:04:48.100 --> 01:04:52.695 align:middle line:84%
The type l sender in equilibrium
is choosing el, which is 0.

01:04:52.695 --> 01:04:54.320 align:middle line:84%
And we have to check
that that's better

01:04:54.320 --> 01:04:57.600 align:middle line:84%
for them than deviating to
any other level of education.

01:04:57.600 --> 01:04:59.880 align:middle line:84%
Similarly, the high
type, in equilibrium,

01:04:59.880 --> 01:05:02.420 align:middle line:84%
is choosing eh, which
is strictly positive.

01:05:02.420 --> 01:05:04.080 align:middle line:84%
And we have to check
that they prefer

01:05:04.080 --> 01:05:06.360 align:middle line:84%
eh to any other
level of education.

01:05:06.360 --> 01:05:08.340 align:middle line:84%
Now I think this can be
overwhelming at first

01:05:08.340 --> 01:05:10.560 align:middle line:84%
because there's so many
other levels of education

01:05:10.560 --> 01:05:12.120 align:middle line:84%
that people might
choose, but just

01:05:12.120 --> 01:05:15.600 align:middle line:84%
like before, given
this wage function,

01:05:15.600 --> 01:05:18.780 align:middle line:84%
I think there's only one
really candidate deviation.

01:05:18.780 --> 01:05:21.120 align:middle line:84%
I think most of the deviations
are clearly not going

01:05:21.120 --> 01:05:22.380 align:middle line:90%
to be the optimal deviation.

01:05:22.380 --> 01:05:25.280 align:middle line:90%
So let's say I'm a low type.

01:05:25.280 --> 01:05:26.240 align:middle line:90%
Here I am.

01:05:26.240 --> 01:05:27.480 align:middle line:90%
I'm down here.

01:05:27.480 --> 01:05:29.172 align:middle line:90%
I'm getting a wage of l.

01:05:29.172 --> 01:05:31.380 align:middle line:84%
I see that this is what the
wage function looks like,

01:05:31.380 --> 01:05:33.480 align:middle line:84%
and this is the choice
that people actually face

01:05:33.480 --> 01:05:36.320 align:middle line:90%
when they design their lives.

01:05:36.320 --> 01:05:41.600 align:middle line:84%
What level of education might
I be tempted to deviate to?

01:05:41.600 --> 01:05:42.100 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

01:05:42.100 --> 01:05:42.960 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: e of h?

01:05:42.960 --> 01:05:43.820 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: eh, right?

01:05:43.820 --> 01:05:45.760 align:middle line:84%
None of the other
deviations make sense.

01:05:45.760 --> 01:05:48.440 align:middle line:84%
If I deviate to
here, I'm paying more

01:05:48.440 --> 01:05:50.640 align:middle line:84%
and I'm not getting
any wage return.

01:05:50.640 --> 01:05:53.427 align:middle line:84%
If I deviate to here, well,
I am getting a wage return,

01:05:53.427 --> 01:05:55.260 align:middle line:84%
but I'm getting the
same return I could have

01:05:55.260 --> 01:05:56.980 align:middle line:90%
gotten by just going to eh.

01:05:56.980 --> 01:06:00.002 align:middle line:84%
So if I want to deviate, that's
really the key deviation.

01:06:00.002 --> 01:06:01.460 align:middle line:84%
And similarly, now
let's put myself

01:06:01.460 --> 01:06:07.460 align:middle line:84%
in the position of someone of
a high type who's choosing eh.

01:06:07.460 --> 01:06:10.380 align:middle line:84%
They're here, and they're
looking over the graph.

01:06:10.380 --> 01:06:14.460 align:middle line:84%
What is the only reasonable
or tempting deviation

01:06:14.460 --> 01:06:15.440 align:middle line:90%
for the high type?

01:06:15.440 --> 01:06:20.980 align:middle line:90%


01:06:20.980 --> 01:06:21.480 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

01:06:21.480 --> 01:06:22.620 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: el?

01:06:22.620 --> 01:06:25.260 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: Exactly. el,
which, in this case, is 0.

01:06:25.260 --> 01:06:26.780 align:middle line:90%
So el, for the same reason.

01:06:26.780 --> 01:06:28.123 align:middle line:90%
I'm already getting a wage of h.

01:06:28.123 --> 01:06:30.040 align:middle line:84%
There's no reason for
me to go to school more.

01:06:30.040 --> 01:06:32.457 align:middle line:84%
I don't like school, and it's
not going to raise my wages,

01:06:32.457 --> 01:06:34.180 align:middle line:90%
according to this strategy.

01:06:34.180 --> 01:06:35.620 align:middle line:84%
If I go to less
school, well, I'm

01:06:35.620 --> 01:06:38.042 align:middle line:84%
going to get a big cut in
wages, but I can go all the way

01:06:38.042 --> 01:06:40.500 align:middle line:84%
down to here and I'm still
going to get the same wage of l.

01:06:40.500 --> 01:06:42.940 align:middle line:84%
So I might as well choose the
lowest education level that

01:06:42.940 --> 01:06:45.740 align:middle line:90%
ensures that I get that wage.

01:06:45.740 --> 01:06:47.720 align:middle line:84%
So now let's write
down these conditions.

01:06:47.720 --> 01:07:13.030 align:middle line:90%


01:07:13.030 --> 01:07:15.270 align:middle line:84%
So really, what we
need to check is--

01:07:15.270 --> 01:07:17.610 align:middle line:84%
and sometimes I write it
like this, in shorthand.

01:07:17.610 --> 01:07:25.610 align:middle line:90%


01:07:25.610 --> 01:07:29.750 align:middle line:84%
So what I mean by
this is, this, I say,

01:07:29.750 --> 01:07:31.590 align:middle line:90%
this means type h prefers.

01:07:31.590 --> 01:07:32.312 align:middle line:90%
This is a weak.

01:07:32.312 --> 01:07:33.770 align:middle line:84%
Maybe I'll say
something like this.

01:07:33.770 --> 01:07:35.670 align:middle line:84%
So I need to check
is if I'm type h,

01:07:35.670 --> 01:07:38.950 align:middle line:84%
I prefer choosing an
education level of eh

01:07:38.950 --> 01:07:41.270 align:middle line:90%
to an education level of el.

01:07:41.270 --> 01:07:43.770 align:middle line:84%
Of course, I have to prefer it
to any other education level,

01:07:43.770 --> 01:07:46.630 align:middle line:84%
but we said this is really
the deviation I'm tempted by.

01:07:46.630 --> 01:07:48.890 align:middle line:84%
And then we have to check
if I'm a low type l,

01:07:48.890 --> 01:07:52.170 align:middle line:84%
I weakly prefer my
own education level el

01:07:52.170 --> 01:07:55.570 align:middle line:84%
to deviating to the
education level eh.

01:07:55.570 --> 01:07:58.350 align:middle line:84%
Now in general, I have to worry
about every other deviation,

01:07:58.350 --> 01:08:00.130 align:middle line:84%
but because of the
special structure

01:08:00.130 --> 01:08:02.830 align:middle line:84%
we've used when we define
this wage function,

01:08:02.830 --> 01:08:04.670 align:middle line:84%
we only have to check
these two conditions.

01:08:04.670 --> 01:08:08.170 align:middle line:84%
So let's just write
this out in math.

01:08:08.170 --> 01:08:11.370 align:middle line:84%
If I'm a high type and
I choose eh, then I'm

01:08:11.370 --> 01:08:13.890 align:middle line:84%
going to get a wage
of h, but then I'm

01:08:13.890 --> 01:08:19.410 align:middle line:84%
going to have to pay
1 over h eh squared.

01:08:19.410 --> 01:08:20.850 align:middle line:90%
Maybe let's go down here.

01:08:20.850 --> 01:08:23.790 align:middle line:84%
What if I'm a low type-- no,
we'll just go through it.

01:08:23.790 --> 01:08:25.050 align:middle line:90%
Let's see.

01:08:25.050 --> 01:08:31.510 align:middle line:84%
Now if I deviate to
el, well, then I get l.

01:08:31.510 --> 01:08:32.510 align:middle line:90%
Maybe I'll write it out.

01:08:32.510 --> 01:08:38.510 align:middle line:84%
It's 1 over h el squared, but
that's just l because el is 0.

01:08:38.510 --> 01:08:47.029 align:middle line:90%


01:08:47.029 --> 01:08:53.710 align:middle line:84%
And I think as usual, let's
simplify this a little bit.

01:08:53.710 --> 01:08:58.810 align:middle line:84%
That is, h minus l is
greater than or equal to--

01:08:58.810 --> 01:09:07.388 align:middle line:90%


01:09:07.388 --> 01:09:08.930 align:middle line:84%
and this has the
same interpretation.

01:09:08.930 --> 01:09:11.450 align:middle line:84%
Now the wage
premium is different

01:09:11.450 --> 01:09:12.930 align:middle line:90%
in the separating equilibrium.

01:09:12.930 --> 01:09:15.229 align:middle line:84%
The wage premium
is now h minus l.

01:09:15.229 --> 01:09:18.149 align:middle line:84%
And what this condition
says is the wage premium

01:09:18.149 --> 01:09:22.630 align:middle line:84%
is higher than the cost of
education for the high type.

01:09:22.630 --> 01:09:25.550 align:middle line:84%
So going to college and
getting an education of eh

01:09:25.550 --> 01:09:28.990 align:middle line:84%
is worth it for the high type
because their cost of doing that

01:09:28.990 --> 01:09:31.069 align:middle line:84%
is weakly smaller
than the wage premium

01:09:31.069 --> 01:09:33.550 align:middle line:90%
they'll get from doing that.

01:09:33.550 --> 01:09:36.630 align:middle line:84%
Now let's look at the same
condition for the low type.

01:09:36.630 --> 01:09:39.830 align:middle line:84%
So for the low type, well, if
they choose their equilibrium

01:09:39.830 --> 01:09:42.310 align:middle line:84%
education level of 0,
they don't pay anything

01:09:42.310 --> 01:09:44.370 align:middle line:90%
and they get a wage of l.

01:09:44.370 --> 01:09:46.890 align:middle line:84%
So we get-- and
again, maybe I'll--

01:09:46.890 --> 01:09:49.450 align:middle line:84%
it's l, but if we really
write it out, it's this.

01:09:49.450 --> 01:09:53.010 align:middle line:90%


01:09:53.010 --> 01:09:56.490 align:middle line:84%
Where this just second term
disappears because el is 0.

01:09:56.490 --> 01:09:59.990 align:middle line:84%
And this has to be greater
than or equal to what?

01:09:59.990 --> 01:10:03.630 align:middle line:84%
Well, if they deviate and choose
a high level of education,

01:10:03.630 --> 01:10:06.010 align:middle line:84%
they're going to get the
wage h, but then they

01:10:06.010 --> 01:10:11.650 align:middle line:90%
have to pay 1 over l eh squared.

01:10:11.650 --> 01:10:14.470 align:middle line:90%
And again, let's simplify.

01:10:14.470 --> 01:10:28.330 align:middle line:90%


01:10:28.330 --> 01:10:30.390 align:middle line:90%
And what does this say in words?

01:10:30.390 --> 01:10:43.070 align:middle line:90%


01:10:43.070 --> 01:10:44.310 align:middle line:90%
Can we interpret this?

01:10:44.310 --> 01:10:45.150 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

01:10:45.150 --> 01:10:48.510 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: The wage premium
is smaller than the cost

01:10:48.510 --> 01:10:53.050 align:middle line:84%
of, I guess, getting
education eh for the low type.

01:10:53.050 --> 01:10:54.550 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: For the
low type, exactly.

01:10:54.550 --> 01:10:55.973 align:middle line:90%
So-- and this is--

01:10:55.973 --> 01:10:58.390 align:middle line:84%
if you recall, it's similar
when we talked about the essay

01:10:58.390 --> 01:11:00.710 align:middle line:84%
problem last week, and we
said the cost of the essay

01:11:00.710 --> 01:11:04.110 align:middle line:84%
has to be cheap enough that
the excited students take it,

01:11:04.110 --> 01:11:06.610 align:middle line:84%
but expensive enough that the
less excited students take it,

01:11:06.610 --> 01:11:08.110 align:middle line:84%
that's exactly
what's going on here.

01:11:08.110 --> 01:11:09.990 align:middle line:84%
In order to sustain a
separating equilibrium,

01:11:09.990 --> 01:11:11.890 align:middle line:84%
because it's a
separating equilibrium,

01:11:11.890 --> 01:11:13.690 align:middle line:84%
the wage premium is
going to be h minus l,

01:11:13.690 --> 01:11:16.453 align:middle line:84%
because high education fully
reveals you're a high type,

01:11:16.453 --> 01:11:18.370 align:middle line:84%
low education fully
reveals you're a low type,

01:11:18.370 --> 01:11:19.990 align:middle line:84%
so that defines--
the fact that it's

01:11:19.990 --> 01:11:22.590 align:middle line:84%
a separating equilibrium
defines what the wage premium is

01:11:22.590 --> 01:11:23.430 align:middle line:90%
going to be.

01:11:23.430 --> 01:11:25.263 align:middle line:84%
But then we have to
check that it's actually

01:11:25.263 --> 01:11:27.950 align:middle line:84%
optimal for each type to follow
what they're supposed to do.

01:11:27.950 --> 01:11:29.750 align:middle line:90%
In this equilibrium.

01:11:29.750 --> 01:11:32.470 align:middle line:84%
The high type people are
supposed to get educated,

01:11:32.470 --> 01:11:36.230 align:middle line:84%
so the level of education
eh must be low enough

01:11:36.230 --> 01:11:38.790 align:middle line:84%
that it's worth it for high
ability or high type people

01:11:38.790 --> 01:11:41.170 align:middle line:84%
to choose to get that
education because the wage

01:11:41.170 --> 01:11:43.770 align:middle line:84%
premium outweighs the
cost for those types.

01:11:43.770 --> 01:11:47.110 align:middle line:84%
At the same time, the level of
education must be high enough.

01:11:47.110 --> 01:11:49.010 align:middle line:84%
If it was too low,
then even the low types

01:11:49.010 --> 01:11:50.130 align:middle line:84%
would also choose
that education,

01:11:50.130 --> 01:11:52.430 align:middle line:84%
and we would no longer have
a separating equilibrium.

01:11:52.430 --> 01:11:54.570 align:middle line:84%
So the level of education
must, at the same time,

01:11:54.570 --> 01:11:56.510 align:middle line:84%
be high enough that
for the low types,

01:11:56.510 --> 01:11:59.970 align:middle line:84%
it's not worth going to
education-- going to school

01:11:59.970 --> 01:12:04.090 align:middle line:84%
because the cost exceeds the
wage premium that they get.

01:12:04.090 --> 01:12:07.790 align:middle line:84%
And if we do this, we can get a
formula for what eh has to be.

01:12:07.790 --> 01:12:09.910 align:middle line:90%
So maybe this is helpful.

01:12:09.910 --> 01:12:17.080 align:middle line:84%
Let's pull the h over
here, and what we get is eh

01:12:17.080 --> 01:12:23.290 align:middle line:84%
needs to be between
h, h minus l,

01:12:23.290 --> 01:12:28.410 align:middle line:84%
and it has to be above
l times h minus l.

01:12:28.410 --> 01:12:30.090 align:middle line:84%
And let's just check
that this works.

01:12:30.090 --> 01:12:32.890 align:middle line:84%
Well, because l
is smaller than h,

01:12:32.890 --> 01:12:35.490 align:middle line:84%
this inequality does
indeed-- is indeed

01:12:35.490 --> 01:12:39.750 align:middle line:84%
satisfied for a
certain range of types.

01:12:39.750 --> 01:12:45.870 align:middle line:90%
I think it's helpful to compare.

01:12:45.870 --> 01:12:51.510 align:middle line:84%
So this is-- let's
compare what happened

01:12:51.510 --> 01:12:54.450 align:middle line:90%
in the pooling equilibrium.

01:12:54.450 --> 01:12:55.690 align:middle line:90%
So this is separating.

01:12:55.690 --> 01:13:00.070 align:middle line:90%


01:13:00.070 --> 01:13:01.910 align:middle line:84%
So in separating,
the low types got

01:13:01.910 --> 01:13:04.590 align:middle line:84%
no education and the
high types got eh.

01:13:04.590 --> 01:13:08.350 align:middle line:84%
In the pooling equilibrium,
both types got e star.

01:13:08.350 --> 01:13:12.210 align:middle line:84%
And e star satisfied
the following property.

01:13:12.210 --> 01:13:21.750 align:middle line:90%


01:13:21.750 --> 01:13:24.390 align:middle line:90%
So how do these compare?

01:13:24.390 --> 01:13:30.550 align:middle line:84%
If we look at these bounds, how
does the pooling equilibrium

01:13:30.550 --> 01:13:31.050 align:middle line:90%
compare?

01:13:31.050 --> 01:13:34.770 align:middle line:84%
How does e star compare to eh if
we look at these inequalities?

01:13:34.770 --> 01:13:36.800 align:middle line:90%
Let's look.

01:13:36.800 --> 01:13:40.260 align:middle line:84%
Mu is an average
between h and l.

01:13:40.260 --> 01:13:42.840 align:middle line:84%
So mu is certainly
smaller than h.

01:13:42.840 --> 01:13:46.357 align:middle line:90%
So this is smaller than this.

01:13:46.357 --> 01:13:48.440 align:middle line:84%
So we see a fundamental
difference between the two

01:13:48.440 --> 01:13:49.840 align:middle line:90%
kinds of equilibria.

01:13:49.840 --> 01:13:52.440 align:middle line:84%
In the separating
equilibrium, the low types

01:13:52.440 --> 01:13:56.640 align:middle line:84%
get no education and the high
types get a lot of education.

01:13:56.640 --> 01:13:58.840 align:middle line:84%
In the pooling
equilibrium, both types

01:13:58.840 --> 01:14:01.420 align:middle line:84%
get this lower intermediate
level of education.

01:14:01.420 --> 01:14:05.440 align:middle line:84%
That's e star is must be
strictly lower than eh.

01:14:05.440 --> 01:14:07.020 align:middle line:90%
So we have two worlds.

01:14:07.020 --> 01:14:09.540 align:middle line:84%
We have one world-- and
they're both equilibria.

01:14:09.540 --> 01:14:11.800 align:middle line:84%
There's one world
where getting educated

01:14:11.800 --> 01:14:14.960 align:middle line:84%
conveys a lot of
benefits, and people--

01:14:14.960 --> 01:14:18.280 align:middle line:84%
or I should say, getting very
highly educated conveys a lot

01:14:18.280 --> 01:14:19.080 align:middle line:90%
of benefits.

01:14:19.080 --> 01:14:20.380 align:middle line:90%
People pay a huge cost.

01:14:20.380 --> 01:14:22.660 align:middle line:84%
The high types pay a huge
cost to get highly educated,

01:14:22.660 --> 01:14:25.120 align:middle line:90%
and they get a huge return.

01:14:25.120 --> 01:14:26.960 align:middle line:84%
There's also another
equilibrium we

01:14:26.960 --> 01:14:30.180 align:middle line:84%
could be in where people
don't get as much as educated,

01:14:30.180 --> 01:14:33.000 align:middle line:84%
there's not a huge benefit to
education, but everyone gets it.

01:14:33.000 --> 01:14:36.660 align:middle line:84%
And I think these actually are
consistent with-- you might

01:14:36.660 --> 01:14:38.780 align:middle line:84%
imagine in different
countries, we might kind of

01:14:38.780 --> 01:14:40.697 align:middle line:84%
reach different equilibria
where education has

01:14:40.697 --> 01:14:44.420 align:middle line:90%
a different role in society.

01:14:44.420 --> 01:14:46.202 align:middle line:90%
Both of these are consistent.

01:14:46.202 --> 01:14:47.660 align:middle line:84%
Everyone's behaving
optimally given

01:14:47.660 --> 01:14:50.560 align:middle line:84%
what everyone else
is doing, but here,

01:14:50.560 --> 01:14:52.592 align:middle line:84%
the beliefs that
the market forms

01:14:52.592 --> 01:14:54.300 align:middle line:84%
about your education
and this equilibrium

01:14:54.300 --> 01:14:55.860 align:middle line:84%
are different-- sorry,
the beliefs, the market

01:14:55.860 --> 01:14:57.780 align:middle line:84%
forms about your ability
given your education

01:14:57.780 --> 01:14:59.620 align:middle line:84%
are different than the
beliefs the market forms

01:14:59.620 --> 01:15:01.120 align:middle line:84%
in this equilibrium,
and then people

01:15:01.120 --> 01:15:03.500 align:middle line:84%
are responding accordingly,
and it's mutually

01:15:03.500 --> 01:15:04.720 align:middle line:90%
consistent in each case.

01:15:04.720 --> 01:15:07.580 align:middle line:90%


01:15:07.580 --> 01:15:10.080 align:middle line:84%
Let me maybe just
do one final thing.

01:15:10.080 --> 01:15:14.980 align:middle line:84%
I think it's sometimes helpful
to visualize these constraints

01:15:14.980 --> 01:15:16.140 align:middle line:90%
geometrically.

01:15:16.140 --> 01:15:18.180 align:middle line:84%
So I've written
out the constraint

01:15:18.180 --> 01:15:20.040 align:middle line:84%
for the separating
equilibrium up here,

01:15:20.040 --> 01:15:22.740 align:middle line:84%
but I want to try
to visualize this.

01:15:22.740 --> 01:15:28.070 align:middle line:84%
And I think a good way to
visualize this is to say,

01:15:28.070 --> 01:15:29.820 align:middle line:84%
well, we want to look
at something that is

01:15:29.820 --> 01:15:31.343 align:middle line:90%
called an indifference curve.

01:15:31.343 --> 01:15:33.260 align:middle line:84%
And if you've studied
other economics classes,

01:15:33.260 --> 01:15:34.320 align:middle line:90%
you would have seen this.

01:15:34.320 --> 01:15:36.880 align:middle line:84%
But what we want to say is,
well, we're in the plain

01:15:36.880 --> 01:15:40.720 align:middle line:84%
here where the horizontal axis
is your level of education

01:15:40.720 --> 01:15:43.240 align:middle line:84%
and the vertical
axis is your wage.

01:15:43.240 --> 01:15:49.200 align:middle line:84%
And we want to ask, what are the
pairs of wage and education that

01:15:49.200 --> 01:15:52.280 align:middle line:90%
give you the same utility?

01:15:52.280 --> 01:15:53.980 align:middle line:84%
Well let's write
the formula down.

01:15:53.980 --> 01:15:58.560 align:middle line:84%
So for the low
type, or for type t,

01:15:58.560 --> 01:16:02.480 align:middle line:84%
an indifference curve would
have the form, say, u bar equals

01:16:02.480 --> 01:16:07.520 align:middle line:90%
wage minus 1 over t e squared.

01:16:07.520 --> 01:16:10.760 align:middle line:84%
So what I'm saying is I want
to look at all the pairs

01:16:10.760 --> 01:16:15.600 align:middle line:84%
we that give type t
the utility u bar.

01:16:15.600 --> 01:16:18.160 align:middle line:84%
And if I solve-- if I
write this out, what I get

01:16:18.160 --> 01:16:24.980 align:middle line:84%
is w equals u bar plus
1 over t e squared.

01:16:24.980 --> 01:16:29.640 align:middle line:90%


01:16:29.640 --> 01:16:31.860 align:middle line:84%
So what this tells
me is w is going

01:16:31.860 --> 01:16:35.440 align:middle line:84%
to be an increasing quadratic
function of e squared.

01:16:35.440 --> 01:16:37.023 align:middle line:90%
So let's try to write this down.

01:16:37.023 --> 01:16:38.440 align:middle line:84%
It might look
something like this.

01:16:38.440 --> 01:16:43.380 align:middle line:90%


01:16:43.380 --> 01:16:47.500 align:middle line:84%
What this indicates is the set--
what I'm saying is if I moved

01:16:47.500 --> 01:16:52.060 align:middle line:84%
along this curve, the
utility that I would get--

01:16:52.060 --> 01:16:53.580 align:middle line:90%
say I'm the low type here--

01:16:53.580 --> 01:16:55.458 align:middle line:90%
is the same along this curve.

01:16:55.458 --> 01:16:58.000 align:middle line:84%
Because if I move a little bit
to the right along this curve,

01:16:58.000 --> 01:17:00.480 align:middle line:84%
I'm getting more educated,
that's more costly,

01:17:00.480 --> 01:17:03.140 align:middle line:84%
but my wage is also increasing
at just the right rate

01:17:03.140 --> 01:17:05.940 align:middle line:84%
to ensure that my
utility stays constant.

01:17:05.940 --> 01:17:07.940 align:middle line:84%
And then we could draw
another utility curve.

01:17:07.940 --> 01:17:09.980 align:middle line:84%
By shifting the
value of u bar, we

01:17:09.980 --> 01:17:13.620 align:middle line:84%
could draw another utility
curve like this and another one

01:17:13.620 --> 01:17:14.700 align:middle line:90%
like this.

01:17:14.700 --> 01:17:17.740 align:middle line:84%
So what this says is, these
all give me the same utility,

01:17:17.740 --> 01:17:19.200 align:middle line:84%
these all give me
the same utility,

01:17:19.200 --> 01:17:21.657 align:middle line:84%
but that utility is going to
be higher than this utility.

01:17:21.657 --> 01:17:23.740 align:middle line:84%
And similarly, these all
give me the same utility,

01:17:23.740 --> 01:17:26.420 align:middle line:84%
but a higher utility still
than the previous case.

01:17:26.420 --> 01:17:29.520 align:middle line:90%


01:17:29.520 --> 01:17:32.640 align:middle line:84%
Now, let's say these
are the indifference

01:17:32.640 --> 01:17:36.340 align:middle line:90%
curves for the low types.

01:17:36.340 --> 01:17:39.290 align:middle line:90%


01:17:39.290 --> 01:17:41.040 align:middle line:84%
Now I want to draw the
indifference curves

01:17:41.040 --> 01:17:43.200 align:middle line:90%
for the high types.

01:17:43.200 --> 01:17:44.137 align:middle line:90%
And this is tricky.

01:17:44.137 --> 01:17:45.720 align:middle line:84%
Are the high types
indifference curves

01:17:45.720 --> 01:17:47.640 align:middle line:84%
going to be steeper
than the low type

01:17:47.640 --> 01:17:49.727 align:middle line:90%
or shallower than the low type?

01:17:49.727 --> 01:17:51.560 align:middle line:84%
This is-- we can do the
algebra, but I think

01:17:51.560 --> 01:17:55.477 align:middle line:90%
it's good to think it through.

01:17:55.477 --> 01:17:57.060 align:middle line:84%
So they're also going
to be quadratic.

01:17:57.060 --> 01:17:59.380 align:middle line:84%
They're going to
have a similar shape.

01:17:59.380 --> 01:18:02.960 align:middle line:90%


01:18:02.960 --> 01:18:04.540 align:middle line:90%
Is that shallower or steeper?

01:18:04.540 --> 01:18:06.200 align:middle line:90%
What do you think?

01:18:06.200 --> 01:18:07.300 align:middle line:90%
You have a half-chance.

01:18:07.300 --> 01:18:12.052 align:middle line:90%


01:18:12.052 --> 01:18:13.260 align:middle line:90%
Well, let's think it through.

01:18:13.260 --> 01:18:14.380 align:middle line:90%
What does this curve mean?

01:18:14.380 --> 01:18:18.120 align:middle line:84%
It tells me, if I get
this much more education,

01:18:18.120 --> 01:18:20.680 align:middle line:84%
I have to get paid this much
more to compensate me for it

01:18:20.680 --> 01:18:22.288 align:middle line:90%
and keep me indifferent.

01:18:22.288 --> 01:18:24.080 align:middle line:84%
So it is a high type
need to be compensated

01:18:24.080 --> 01:18:28.940 align:middle line:84%
more or less for the
same level of education.

01:18:28.940 --> 01:18:30.620 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: More.

01:18:30.620 --> 01:18:32.700 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: Actually less
because a high type

01:18:32.700 --> 01:18:35.420 align:middle line:90%
finds education less costly.

01:18:35.420 --> 01:18:38.940 align:middle line:84%
So if I'm really good at school,
and I go to school a little bit

01:18:38.940 --> 01:18:40.860 align:middle line:84%
more, I don't need
to get paid too much

01:18:40.860 --> 01:18:42.240 align:middle line:90%
more to keep me indifferent.

01:18:42.240 --> 01:18:44.900 align:middle line:84%
If I'm really good at school,
I can go to school for 20 years

01:18:44.900 --> 01:18:47.060 align:middle line:84%
and you just need to
pay me a little bit.

01:18:47.060 --> 01:18:52.900 align:middle line:84%
So here, I'm going
to get curves like--

01:18:52.900 --> 01:18:55.860 align:middle line:90%
say something like this.

01:18:55.860 --> 01:18:57.440 align:middle line:90%
No, these should not intersect.

01:18:57.440 --> 01:18:59.820 align:middle line:90%
Sorry.

01:18:59.820 --> 01:19:04.700 align:middle line:90%
Something like that.

01:19:04.700 --> 01:19:06.080 align:middle line:90%
Let me-- I forget this last one.

01:19:06.080 --> 01:19:08.940 align:middle line:90%


01:19:08.940 --> 01:19:11.320 align:middle line:84%
So these are the indifference
curves for the high types.

01:19:11.320 --> 01:19:20.220 align:middle line:90%


01:19:20.220 --> 01:19:22.700 align:middle line:84%
And in fact, using
these curves, we

01:19:22.700 --> 01:19:25.190 align:middle line:84%
can exactly
represent graphically

01:19:25.190 --> 01:19:27.590 align:middle line:84%
the levels of education
that could support

01:19:27.590 --> 01:19:29.030 align:middle line:90%
a separating equilibrium.

01:19:29.030 --> 01:19:33.490 align:middle line:84%
So it's exactly going
to be this range here.

01:19:33.490 --> 01:19:48.710 align:middle line:84%
Do I have-- and let's
just see it in words.

01:19:48.710 --> 01:19:53.590 align:middle line:84%
This dotted line here is the
indifference curve that passes

01:19:53.590 --> 01:19:56.310 align:middle line:90%
through 0l for the low type.

01:19:56.310 --> 01:19:59.590 align:middle line:84%
So these are the wage-education
level pairs that make the low

01:19:59.590 --> 01:20:04.750 align:middle line:84%
type indifferent with 0l, with
no education and a wage of l.

01:20:04.750 --> 01:20:07.310 align:middle line:84%
So if I want to have a
separating equilibrium,

01:20:07.310 --> 01:20:10.350 align:middle line:84%
I better make this
eh high enough

01:20:10.350 --> 01:20:12.430 align:middle line:84%
that the low type doesn't
want to deviate to eh.

01:20:12.430 --> 01:20:15.030 align:middle line:84%
And that precisely means
eh has to be farther

01:20:15.030 --> 01:20:18.830 align:middle line:84%
to the right of this point
because if eh is over here, that

01:20:18.830 --> 01:20:21.430 align:middle line:84%
tells me the low
type strictly prefers

01:20:21.430 --> 01:20:23.690 align:middle line:84%
to get no education
and a wage of l

01:20:23.690 --> 01:20:27.490 align:middle line:84%
than to get this level of
education and a wage of h.

01:20:27.490 --> 01:20:30.890 align:middle line:84%
On the other hand, the
high type must weakly

01:20:30.890 --> 01:20:33.990 align:middle line:84%
prefer to get high
education than no education.

01:20:33.990 --> 01:20:37.130 align:middle line:90%


01:20:37.130 --> 01:20:39.760 align:middle line:90%
Ah, I misdrew this.

01:20:39.760 --> 01:20:41.510 align:middle line:84%
It's going to go all
the way over to here.

01:20:41.510 --> 01:20:42.010 align:middle line:90%
Sorry.

01:20:42.010 --> 01:20:49.810 align:middle line:90%


01:20:49.810 --> 01:20:54.210 align:middle line:84%
So the high types indifference
curve that passes through 0l

01:20:54.210 --> 01:20:58.650 align:middle line:90%
goes all the way up to here.

01:20:58.650 --> 01:21:00.830 align:middle line:84%
So just to maybe go
through it one last time,

01:21:00.830 --> 01:21:02.410 align:middle line:90%
let's make sure we understand.

01:21:02.410 --> 01:21:03.970 align:middle line:84%
If I could get a
level of education

01:21:03.970 --> 01:21:06.490 align:middle line:84%
here and get paid
a wage of h, that

01:21:06.490 --> 01:21:09.490 align:middle line:84%
would be better than no
education and a wage of l

01:21:09.490 --> 01:21:12.050 align:middle line:90%
for both high and low types.

01:21:12.050 --> 01:21:14.890 align:middle line:84%
So that couldn't be part of
a separating equilibrium.

01:21:14.890 --> 01:21:17.330 align:middle line:84%
If I chose eh to
be way over here,

01:21:17.330 --> 01:21:20.650 align:middle line:84%
then getting this level of
education and a wage of h would

01:21:20.650 --> 01:21:23.590 align:middle line:90%
be worse than 0l for both types.

01:21:23.590 --> 01:21:25.590 align:middle line:84%
The only way I can have
a separating equilibrium

01:21:25.590 --> 01:21:27.550 align:middle line:84%
is if I lie exactly
in this range.

01:21:27.550 --> 01:21:29.310 align:middle line:84%
And if I lie in
that range, I ensure

01:21:29.310 --> 01:21:33.650 align:middle line:84%
that this additional education
is worth it for the high type,

01:21:33.650 --> 01:21:35.830 align:middle line:84%
but not worth it
for the low type.

01:21:35.830 --> 01:21:38.830 align:middle line:84%
And the crucial point here is
I'm looking at the indifference

01:21:38.830 --> 01:21:42.470 align:middle line:84%
curves that pass
through the point 0l.

01:21:42.470 --> 01:21:44.910 align:middle line:84%
So these are considering
the pairs of education

01:21:44.910 --> 01:21:46.330 align:middle line:90%
and wages that--

01:21:46.330 --> 01:21:47.810 align:middle line:84%
So let me just--
as I'm concluding,

01:21:47.810 --> 01:21:51.070 align:middle line:84%
let me say it smoothly
in words one last time.

01:21:51.070 --> 01:21:53.510 align:middle line:84%
These are the pairs
of wages in education

01:21:53.510 --> 01:21:56.430 align:middle line:84%
that make the high type
indifferent with this point,

01:21:56.430 --> 01:21:58.388 align:middle line:84%
and these are the pairs
of education and wages

01:21:58.388 --> 01:21:59.430 align:middle line:90%
that make the high type--

01:21:59.430 --> 01:22:01.970 align:middle line:84%
that make the low type
indifferent with this point.

01:22:01.970 --> 01:22:03.718 align:middle line:84%
And everything in
between is going

01:22:03.718 --> 01:22:05.510 align:middle line:84%
to ensure that the high
type finds it worth

01:22:05.510 --> 01:22:08.517 align:middle line:84%
it to get educated and the low
type finds it not worth it.

01:22:08.517 --> 01:22:10.850 align:middle line:84%
We did the algebra, but it's
just good to see the graph,

01:22:10.850 --> 01:22:12.433 align:middle line:84%
and I think on the
problem set, you'll

01:22:12.433 --> 01:22:15.980 align:middle line:84%
be asked to illustrate
this graphically.

01:22:15.980 --> 01:22:20.000 align:middle line:90%